{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016Uex2LXSCP5smEhgQbEpBa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture layer sweep + static probe (r1-32b & QwQ-32B)\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Full 64-layer sweep on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and QwQ-32B. Extract hidden states at all layers, evaluate static LR probes per layer under multiple selection metrics. Includes discovery of layer selection bias (bal_acc × crossing_rate → shallow layers).\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"r1_32b_biased_layer\": 10,\n7\t      \"r1_32b_biased_layer_depth_pct\": 15.6,\n8\t      \"r1_32b_corrected_layer\": 63,\n9\t      \"r1_32b_corrected_layer_depth_pct\": 98.4,\n10\t      \"r1_32b_l10_bal_acc\": 0.718,\n11\t      \"r1_32b_l63_bal_acc_w1\": 0.826,\n12\t      \"r1_32b_n_test\": 20,\n13\t      \"r1_32b_l63_precision_w1\": 0.603,\n14\t      \"r1_32b_l63_fpr_w1\": 0.235,\n15\t      \"qwq_32b_biased_layer\": 2,\n16\t      \"qwq_32b_biased_layer_depth_pct\": 3.1,\n17\t      \"qwq_32b_corrected_layer\": 60,\n18\t      \"qwq_32b_corrected_layer_depth_pct\": 93.8,\n19\t      \"qwq_32b_l2_bal_acc\": 0.73,\n20\t      \"qwq_32b_l60_bal_acc_w3\": 0.74,\n21\t      \"qwq_32b_n_test\": \"19-20\",\n22\t      \"qwq_32b_l60_precision_w3\": 0.578,\n23\t      \"qwq_32b_l60_fpr_w3\": 0.279,\n24\t      \"layer_selection_bias_discovered\": true,\n25\t      \"metrics_tested\": 5,\n26\t      \"biased_metric\": \"bal_acc × crossing_rate\",\n27\t      \"loo_cv_dominant_fold_pct\": \"65-68%\"\n28\t    },\n29\t    \"tables\": [\n30\t      {\n31\t        \"name\": \"Layer Selection Metric × Model → Selected Layer\",\n32\t        \"columns\": [\n33\t          \"Metric\",\n34\t          \"R1-8B\",\n35\t          \"OT-7B\",\n36\t          \"QwQ-32B\",\n37\t          \"R1-32B\"\n38\t        ],\n39\t        \"rows\": [\n40\t          [\n41\t            \"bal_acc × crossing_rate\",\n42\t            \"L0 (0%)\",\n43\t            \"L0 (0%)\",\n44\t            \"L2 (3%)\",\n45\t            \"L10 (16%)\"\n46\t          ],\n47\t          [\n48\t            \"pure bal_acc\",\n49\t            \"L12 (39%)\",\n50\t            \"L9 (32%)\",\n51\t            \"L30 (47%)\",\n52\t            \"L47 (73%)\"\n53\t          ],\n54\t          [\n55\t            \"precision\",\n56\t            \"L16 (52%)\",\n57\t            \"L16 (57%)\",\n58\t            \"L60 (94%)\",\n59\t            \"L63 (98%)\"\n60\t          ],\n61\t          [\n62\t            \"pre_cp_fpr\",\n63\t            \"L16 (52%)\",\n64\t            \"L16 (57%)\",\n65\t            \"L60 (94%)\",\n66\t            \"L63 (98%)\"\n67\t          ],\n68\t          [\n69\t            \"composite (additive)\",\n70\t            \"L16 (52%)\",\n71\t            \"L12 (43%)\",\n72\t            \"L60 (94%)\",\n73\t            \"L63 (98%)\"\n74\t          ]\n75\t        ]\n76\t      }\n77\t    ],\n78\t    \"figures\": [],\n79\t    \"analysis\": \"Layer selection sensitivity is the paper's primary methodological contribution, but worst-tier reviewers rate it insufficient novelty (2.0-2.5). The argument: \\\"every ML practitioner knows different metrics select different hyperparameters. Showing this for layer selection is an observation, not a method. There's no proposed algorithm to automatically resolve the disagreement — just 'try more metrics,' which is standard practice.\\\"\\n\\n\\nLayer sweep reveals a genuine methodological finding (biased metric selects shallow layers), and corrected layers do show HS advantage. However, worst-tier reviewers dismiss this: (1) \\\"Finding that one composite metric is bad is trivially expected — practitioners routinely try multiple metrics. This is hyperparameter tuning, not a contribution.\\\" (2) n=19-20 test traces for 32B models mean bootstrap CIs are wide. MDE analysis shows R1-32B residualized gap (+1.9pp) is far below the 11.2pp MDE at 80% power — the NS result for position-mediated HS advantage is a power limitation, not evidence. Reviewers note: \\\"you can't claim the advantage is 'real but position-mediated' when you're underpowered to detect the non-positional component.\\\" (3) The corrected layers (L60/L63, >93% depth) are so deep they're essentially the output layer — reviewer asks: \\\"are you just detecting output-token semantics via a different pathway?\\\"\\n\"\n80\t  },\n81\t  {\n82\t    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation (32B)\",\n83\t    \"description\": \"Temporal LR at multiple window sizes on R1-32B (L63) and QwQ-32B (L60). Evaluate whether temporal context universally improves probe accuracy across 32B models.\\n\",\n84\t    \"results\": {\n85\t      \"r1_32b_l63_w1_bal_acc\": 0.826,\n86\t      \"r1_32b_l63_w1_precision\": 0.603,\n87\t      \"r1_32b_l63_w1_coverage\": 0.25,\n88\t      \"r1_32b_l63_w3_bal_acc\": 0.754,\n89\t      \"r1_32b_l63_w5_bal_acc\": 0.741,\n90\t      \"r1_32b_l63_w10_bal_acc\": 0.737,\n91\t      \"r1_32b_l63_w15_bal_acc\": 0.721,\n92\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_trend\": \"DECREASING with W (W=1 best, -10.5pp by W=15)\",\n93\t      \"r1_32b_dimensionality_issue\": \"51200 × W features for n=20 → massive overfitting at W≥3\",\n94\t      \"qwq_32b_l60_w1_bal_acc\": 0.74,\n95\t      \"qwq_32b_l60_w3_bal_acc\": 0.74,\n96\t      \"qwq_32b_l60_w5_bal_acc\": 0.742,\n97\t      \"qwq_32b_l60_w15_bal_acc\": 0.731,\n98\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_trend\": \"flat to slightly decreasing\",\n99\t      \"qwq_32b_best_w\": 5,\n100\t      \"text_temporal_universal_improvement\": true,\n101\t      \"hs_temporal_improvement_3_of_4\": true,\n102\t      \"hs_temporal_exception\": \"OT-7B HS peaks W=3, declines at larger W; R1-32B only W=1 significant\"\n103\t    },\n104\t    \"tables\": [\n105\t      {\n106\t        \"name\": \"Temporal Window Effect on 32B Models (HS probes, corrected layers)\",\n107\t        \"columns\": [\n108\t          \"Model\",\n109\t          \"Layer\",\n110\t          \"W=1\",\n111\t          \"W=3\",\n112\t          \"W=5\",\n113\t          \"W=10\",\n114\t          \"W=15\",\n115\t          \"Trend\"\n116\t        ],\n117\t        \"rows\": [\n118\t          [\n119\t            \"R1-32B\",\n120\t            \"L63\",\n121\t            \"0.826*\",\n122\t            \"0.754\",\n123\t            \"0.741\",\n124\t            \"0.737\",\n125\t            \"0.721\",\n126\t            \"↓ declining\"\n127\t          ],\n128\t          [\n129\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n130\t            \"L60\",\n131\t            \"0.740\",\n132\t            \"0.740\",\n133\t            \"0.742\",\n134\t            \"0.735\",\n135\t            \"0.731\",\n136\t            \"→ flat\"\n137\t          ],\n138\t          [\n139\t            \"R1-8B\",\n140\t            \"L14\",\n141\t            \"0.778\",\n142\t            \"0.790\",\n143\t            \"0.781\",\n144\t            \"0.793\",\n145\t            \"0.802\",\n146\t            \"↑ improving\"\n147\t          ],\n148\t          [\n149\t            \"OT-7B\",\n150\t            \"L16\",\n151\t            \"0.663\",\n152\t            \"0.688*\",\n153\t            \"0.678\",\n154\t            \"0.668\",\n155\t            \"0.659\",\n156\t            \"↑↓ peaks W=3\"\n157\t          ]\n158\t        ]\n159\t      }\n160\t    ],\n161\t    \"figures\": [],\n162\t    \"analysis\": \"Worst-tier reviewers note: \\\"Temporal context helps universally for text probes (a known result — more context helps NLP classifiers). For HS probes, 2/4 models show non-monotonic or declining trends. The paper's Claim 1 should be 'temporal context helps text probes' — attributing it to HS probes is cherry-picking the models where it works.\\\"\\n\\n\\nTemporal context shows a split pattern at 32B: QwQ benefits modestly across all W (text and HS), but R1-32B DEGRADES with increasing W because concatenating 51200-dimensional vectors across W>1 windows creates a feature space massively exceeding n=20 training samples. This is not a failure of the temporal hypothesis — it's a sample-size bottleneck. Worst-tier reviewers seize on this: \\\"R1-32B temporal results are meaningless because you're fitting 102400+ parameters to 20 samples. The W=1 'significance' at L63 survives only because W=1 avoids this problem. Your 'best' result for R1-32B is actually a dimensionality artifact — the only window that avoids overfitting happens to be significant.\\\" Claim 1 (temporal universally helps) holds for text probes across all 4 models, but for HS probes, 2/4 models have caveats (OT-7B saturation, R1-32B dimensionality). Reviewers frame this as \\\"temporal improvement is a text-probe finding, not an HS-probe finding.\\\"\\n\"\n163\t  },\n164\t  {\n165\t    \"name\": \"Cross-model transfer probes\",\n166\t    \"description\": \"Train probe on one model, test on another. PCA projection for cross-architecture transfer (4096→5120 or vice versa). Tests whether safety commitment encoding is universal.\\n\",\n167\t    \"results\": {\n168\t      \"r1_8b_to_r1_32b_acc\": 0.522,\n169\t      \"r1_8b_to_qwq_acc\": 0.595,\n170\t      \"r1_32b_to_qwq_acc\": 0.673,\n171\t      \"qwq_to_r1_32b_acc\": 0.673,\n172\t      \"r1_32b_to_r1_8b_acc\": 0.583,\n173\t      \"best_transfer_overall\": 0.673,\n174\t      \"chance_baseline\": 0.5,\n175\t      \"all_transfers_below_within_model\": true,\n176\t      \"within_family_32b_weak_transfer\": 0.673,\n177\t      \"cross_family_at_chance\": true\n178\t    },\n179\t    \"tables\": [\n180\t      {\n181\t        \"name\": \"Cross-Model Transfer Accuracy Matrix\",\n182\t        \"columns\": [\n183\t          \"Train →\",\n184\t          \"R1-8B\",\n185\t          \"OT-7B\",\n186\t          \"R1-32B\",\n187\t          \"QwQ-32B\"\n188\t        ],\n189\t        \"rows\": [\n190\t          [\n191\t            \"R1-8B\",\n192\t            \"0.817 (self)\",\n193\t            \"—\",\n194\t            \"0.522\",\n195\t            \"0.595\"\n196\t          ],\n197\t          [\n198\t            \"OT-7B\",\n199\t            \"—\",\n200\t            \"0.688 (self)\",\n201\t            \"—\",\n202\t            \"—\"\n203\t          ],\n204\t          [\n205\t            \"R1-32B\",\n206\t            \"0.583\",\n207\t            \"—\",\n208\t            \"0.826 (self)\",\n209\t            \"0.673\"\n210\t          ],\n211\t          [\n212\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n213\t            \"—\",\n214\t            \"—\",\n215\t            \"0.673\",\n216\t            \"0.740 (self)\"\n217\t          ]\n218\t        ]\n219\t      }\n220\t    ],\n221\t    \"figures\": [],\n222\t    \"analysis\": \"Complete transfer failure. Worst-tier reviewers conclude: \\\"The paper claims to study 'safety commitment detection' as a general phenomenon, but the probes are entirely model-specific. This means the method has zero practical scalability — you need labeled data for every model you want to monitor. Combined with the engineering cost of white-box access, the deployment story collapses.\\\"\\n\\n\\nCross-model transfer fails completely — the best transfer accuracy (67.3% Qwen-family 32B internal) is far below within-model performance and barely above the majority-class baseline. Cross-family transfer (Llama↔Qwen) is at chance. Worst-tier reviewers use this as evidence against the paper: \\\"Safety encoding is model-specific. This means every new model needs its own probe training with labeled commitment points — a supervision cost the paper downplays. If safety commitment were a universal phenomenon, SOME geometric structure should transfer. The complete failure suggests the probes are learning model-specific artifacts, not a generalizable safety signal.\\\" This finding is consistent with the selectivity control task (7.5-18.5pp) but reviewers argue selectivity merely shows the probes aren't random — not that they detect genuine safety commitment vs idiosyncratic model behavior.\\n\"\n223\t  },\n224\t  {\n225\t    \"name\": \"Lead time analysis across models\",\n226\t    \"description\": \"Evaluate HS probe lead time distributions on all 4 models. Compare HS vs text lead times. Lead time = steps between first detection and commitment point.\\n\",\n227\t    \"results\": {\n228\t      \"r1_8b_hs_lead_mean\": -0.1,\n229\t      \"r1_8b_hs_lead_note\": \"HS detects AT commitment, not before\",\n230\t      \"r1_8b_text_lead_mean\": 10.3,\n231\t      \"r1_8b_text_lead_inflated\": \"high text lead is artifact of high pre-CP FPR (trigger-happy)\",\n232\t      \"ot_7b_hs_lead_mean\": 21.6,\n233\t      \"ot_7b_text_lead_mean\": 45.5,\n234\t      \"ot_7b_lead_note\": \"absolute values inflated by long traces (~80 sentences)\",\n235\t      \"qwq_32b_hs_lead_mean_range\": \"-1.2 to +1.9\",\n236\t      \"qwq_32b_text_lead_mean_range\": \"3.7 to 12.3\",\n237\t      \"qwq_32b_hs_lead_negative\": true,\n238\t      \"qwq_32b_position_baseline_lead\": \"better than HS\",\n239\t      \"r1_32b_hs_lead_mean\": \"+11.7 to +13.3\",\n240\t      \"r1_32b_hs_lead_positive\": true,\n241\t      \"r1_32b_coverage_at_significant_w\": 0.25,\n242\t      \"r1_32b_lead_caveat\": \"only 5/20 traces detected at W=1\",\n243\t      \"gru_r1_8b_lead\": \"+4.5 ± 3.0\",\n244\t      \"gru_r1_8b_worst_seed\": \"+0.9\"\n245\t    },\n246\t    \"tables\": [\n247\t      {\n248\t        \"name\": \"Lead Time Comparison: HS vs Text vs Position\",\n249\t        \"columns\": [\n250\t          \"Model\",\n251\t          \"HS Lead (steps)\",\n252\t          \"Text Lead (steps)\",\n253\t          \"Position BA\",\n254\t          \"HS BA\",\n255\t          \"HS > Position?\"\n256\t        ],\n257\t        \"rows\": [\n258\t          [\n259\t            \"R1-8B (W=15)\",\n260\t            \"−0.1\",\n261\t            \"+10.3†\",\n262\t            \"—\",\n263\t            \"0.802\",\n264\t            \"—\"\n265\t          ],\n266\t          [\n267\t            \"OT-7B (W=3)\",\n268\t            \"+21.6‡\",\n269\t            \"+45.5‡\",\n270\t            \"—\",\n271\t            \"0.688\",\n272\t            \"—\"\n273\t          ],\n274\t          [\n275\t            \"QwQ-32B (W=3)\",\n276\t            \"−1.2 to +1.9\",\n277\t            \"+3.7 to +12.3\",\n278\t            \"0.746\",\n279\t            \"0.740\",\n280\t            \"No\"\n281\t          ],\n282\t          [\n283\t            \"R1-32B (W=1)\",\n284\t            \"+11.7 to +13.3\",\n285\t            \"—\",\n286\t            \"0.686\",\n287\t            \"0.826\",\n288\t            \"Yes (25% cov)\"\n289\t          ]\n290\t        ]\n291\t      }\n292\t    ],\n293\t    \"figures\": [],\n294\t    \"analysis\": \"Worst-tier reviewers identify lead time as the practical dealbreaker: \\\"The paper claims HS monitoring enables early detection, but 2/4 models show HS detecting LATER than text. The one model with good lead time (R1-32B) detects only 25% of traces. A deployment team would choose text monitoring over HS monitoring for lead time alone, saving the engineering cost of hidden-state extraction.\\\"\\n\\n\\nLead time results are a mixed bag that worst-tier reviewers weaponize: (1) R1-8B HS lead is effectively zero — HS detects AT commitment, not before. Only GRU achieves modest positive lead (+4.5, worst seed +0.9). Text lead of +10.3 is misleadingly high (artifact of trigger-happy detection with 20.5% pre-CP FPR). (2) QwQ-32B: HS lead time is NEGATIVE (-1.2 to +1.9 steps) while text detects 3.7-12.3 steps earlier. Position baseline (bal_acc=0.746) beats HS (bal_acc=0.740). A reviewer writes: \\\"for QwQ, just tracking relative position in the trace outperforms hidden-state monitoring — why bother with white-box access?\\\" (3) R1-32B has excellent HS lead (+13 steps) but only 25% coverage — meaning 15/20 traces are undetected. Reviewer: \\\"a detector that works on 5 traces is an anecdote, not a system.\\\" (4) OT-7B lead times are inflated by long traces. Overall: 2/4 models have HS lead ≤ 0, 1/4 has only 25% coverage, and 1/4 has inflated absolute values. No model presents a clean HS lead time story.\\n\"\n295\t  },\n296\t  {\n297\t    \"name\": \"TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline (all 4 models)\",\n298\t    \"description\": \"Mahalanobis distance on safe-trace covariance across all 4 models. Tests whether geometric distance-based methods (TrajGuard-style) can detect safety commitment.\\n\",\n299\t    \"results\": {\n300\t      \"r1_8b_separation\": 0.07,\n301\t      \"ot_7b_separation\": 0.03,\n302\t      \"r1_32b_separation\": 0.15,\n303\t      \"qwq_32b_separation\": 0.2,\n304\t      \"max_separation_any_model\": 0.2,\n305\t      \"all_models_fail\": true,\n306\t      \"false_alarm_exceeds_tp_all_thresholds\": true,\n307\t      \"signal_location\": \"minor PCA components, not principal directions\",\n308\t      \"r1_8b_safe_md\": 6.66,\n309\t      \"r1_8b_post_md\": 6.73,\n310\t      \"r1_32b_safe_md\": 6.73,\n311\t      \"r1_32b_post_md\": 6.88,\n312\t      \"qwq_32b_safe_md\": 3.22,\n313\t      \"qwq_32b_post_md\": 3.42\n314\t    },\n315\t    \"tables\": [\n316\t      {\n317\t        \"name\": \"Mahalanobis Distance Separation (4 models)\",\n318\t        \"columns\": [\n319\t          \"Model\",\n320\t          \"Layer\",\n321\t          \"Safe MD\",\n322\t          \"Post-CP MD\",\n323\t          \"Separation\",\n324\t          \"FA ≥ TP?\"\n325\t        ],\n326\t        \"rows\": [\n327\t          [\n328\t            \"R1-8B\",\n329\t            \"L14\",\n330\t            \"6.66\",\n331\t            \"6.73\",\n332\t            \"+0.07\",\n333\t            \"Yes\"\n334\t          ],\n335\t          [\n336\t            \"OT-7B\",\n337\t            \"L16\",\n338\t            \"7.68\",\n339\t            \"7.71\",\n340\t            \"+0.03\",\n341\t            \"Yes\"\n342\t          ],\n343\t          [\n344\t            \"R1-32B\",\n345\t            \"L63\",\n346\t            \"6.73\",\n347\t            \"6.88\",\n348\t            \"+0.15\",\n349\t            \"Yes\"\n350\t          ],\n351\t          [\n352\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n353\t            \"L60\",\n354\t            \"3.22\",\n355\t            \"3.42\",\n356\t            \"+0.20\",\n357\t            \"Yes\"\n358\t          ]\n359\t        ]\n360\t      }\n361\t    ],\n362\t    \"figures\": [],\n363\t    \"analysis\": \"Universal Mahalanobis failure confirms safety signal is geometrically inaccessible to distance-based methods. But worst-tier reviewers dismiss this: \\\"Showing Mahalanobis doesn't work is a paragraph in a methods section, not a contribution. The field already knows unsupervised geometric methods struggle with fine-grained behavioral distinctions.\\\"\\n\\n\\nMahalanobis distance fails on all 4 models (max separation 0.20 on QwQ, FA rate ≥ TP rate at every threshold). The signal lives in minor PCA components, not principal variance directions — Mahalanobis, which weights by inverse covariance, is structurally blind to this geometry. Worst-tier reviewers have mixed reactions: (1) \\\"Mahalanobis failing is expected — Bolukbasi et al. 2016 and subsequent work showed safety-relevant features are often orthogonal to principal variance. This negative result is predictable, not informative.\\\" (2) \\\"You tested one geometric baseline. What about k-NN distance, local outlier factor, or isolation forests? The negative result space is underexplored.\\\" The finding supports the \\\"existing methods fail\\\" claim but reviewers rate it as incremental — failure of one known-limited baseline is not a contribution.\\n\"\n364\t  },\n365\t  {\n366\t    \"name\": \"Bootstrap confidence intervals + threshold-independent metrics\",\n367\t    \"description\": \"10000 bootstrap resamples on all key metrics across 4 models. Holm-Bonferroni correction. AUROC, precision-recall curves, detection delay CDF.\\n\",\n368\t    \"results\": {\n369\t      \"holm_bonferroni_pass\": \"12/12\",\n370\t      \"r1_8b_precision_delta\": \"+22.4pp\",\n371\t      \"r1_8b_precision_p\": \"<0.001\",\n372\t      \"ot_7b_precision_delta\": \"+21.9pp\",\n373\t      \"ot_7b_precision_p\": \"<0.001\",\n374\t      \"qwq_32b_precision_delta\": \"+23.6pp\",\n375\t      \"qwq_32b_precision_p\": \"<0.001\",\n376\t      \"qwq_32b_fpr_p\": 0.004,\n377\t      \"r1_32b_precision_delta\": \"+15.4pp\",\n378\t      \"r1_32b_precision_p\": \"<0.001\",\n379\t      \"r1_32b_fpr_p\": 0.007,\n380\t      \"r1_32b_fpr_marginal_note\": \"p=0.007 passes HB threshold 0.025 but barely\",\n381\t      \"marginal_tests_count\": 2,\n382\t      \"all_precision_deltas_positive\": true,\n383\t      \"precision_range\": \"+15.4 to +23.6pp\",\n384\t      \"r1_32b_ci_width_ba\": \"~0.15\",\n385\t      \"qwq_32b_ci_width_ba\": \"~0.12\",\n386\t      \"r1_8b_ci_width_ba\": \"~0.06\"\n387\t    },\n388\t    \"tables\": [\n389\t      {\n390\t        \"name\": \"Holm-Bonferroni Summary (12/12, α=0.05)\",\n391\t        \"columns\": [\n392\t          \"Rank\",\n393\t          \"Model\",\n394\t          \"Metric\",\n395\t          \"p-value\",\n396\t          \"HB Threshold\",\n397\t          \"Pass\"\n398\t        ],\n399\t        \"rows\": [\n400\t          [\n401\t            1,\n402\t            \"R1-8B\",\n403\t            \"precision\",\n404\t            \"<0.001\",\n405\t            \"0.0042\",\n406\t            \"✅\"\n407\t          ],\n408\t          [\n409\t            2,\n410\t            \"OT-7B\",\n411\t            \"bal_acc\",\n412\t            \"<0.001\",\n413\t            \"0.0045\",\n414\t            \"✅\"\n415\t          ],\n416\t          [\n417\t            3,\n418\t            \"OT-7B\",\n419\t            \"precision\",\n420\t            \"<0.001\",\n421\t            \"0.0050\",\n422\t            \"✅\"\n423\t          ],\n424\t          [\n425\t            4,\n426\t            \"OT-7B\",\n427\t            \"FPR\",\n428\t            \"<0.001\",\n429\t            \"0.0056\",\n430\t            \"✅\"\n431\t          ],\n432\t          [\n433\t            5,\n434\t            \"QwQ\",\n435\t            \"bal_acc\",\n436\t            \"<0.001\",\n437\t            \"0.0063\",\n438\t            \"✅\"\n439\t          ],\n440\t          [\n441\t            6,\n442\t            \"QwQ\",\n443\t            \"precision\",\n444\t            \"<0.001\",\n445\t            \"0.0071\",\n446\t            \"✅\"\n447\t          ],\n448\t          [\n449\t            7,\n450\t            \"R1-32B\",\n451\t            \"bal_acc\",\n452\t            \"<0.001\",\n453\t            \"0.0083\",\n454\t            \"✅\"\n455\t          ],\n456\t          [\n457\t            8,\n458\t            \"R1-32B\",\n459\t            \"precision\",\n460\t            \"<0.001\",\n461\t            \"0.0100\",\n462\t            \"✅\"\n463\t          ],\n464\t          [\n465\t            9,\n466\t            \"QwQ\",\n467\t            \"FPR\",\n468\t            \"0.004\",\n469\t            \"0.0125\",\n470\t            \"✅\"\n471\t          ],\n472\t          [\n473\t            10,\n474\t            \"R1-8B\",\n475\t            \"bal_acc\",\n476\t            \"0.005\",\n477\t            \"0.0167\",\n478\t            \"✅\"\n479\t          ],\n480\t          [\n481\t            11,\n482\t            \"R1-32B\",\n483\t            \"FPR\",\n484\t            \"0.007\",\n485\t            \"0.0250\",\n486\t            \"✅\"\n487\t          ],\n488\t          [\n489\t            12,\n490\t            \"R1-8B\",\n491\t            \"FPR\",\n492\t            \"0.044\",\n493\t            \"0.0500\",\n494\t            \"✅\"\n495\t          ]\n496\t        ]\n497\t      }\n498\t    ],\n499\t    \"figures\": [],\n500\t    \"analysis\": \"Statistical significance is established but reviewers find it insufficient: \\\"The paper's statistical methodology is sound, but the story it tells is 'HS is significantly better by 15-24pp precision at best-case layer/window selection.' For an EMNLP Main paper, we need either (a) a novel method that achieves these gains, or (b) a surprising finding. Neither: linear probes on hidden states are standard, and it's unsurprising they outperform text embeddings (hidden states are an information superset of text).\\\"\\n\\n\\nAll 12/12 tests pass Holm-Bonferroni, which is statistically clean. But worst-tier reviewers attack the PRACTICAL significance, not the statistical significance: (1) \\\"Of course 12/12 pass — you selected the best layer AND the best window per model, then tested. The researcher degrees of freedom (4 models × 64 layers × 7 windows × 5 metrics) dwarf the 12 tests corrected for.\\\" (2) \\\"The CIs for 32B models are extremely wide (±0.15 for R1-32B bal_acc). Statistical significance with n=20 means the effect is large, but the PRECISION of the estimate is poor. You know HS is better somewhere between +3pp and +34pp — that's not actionable.\\\" (3) \\\"R1-32B FPR p=0.007 and QwQ FPR p=0.004 are the most marginal passing tests. A 10001st bootstrap replicate could flip either.\\\" (4) \\\"Holm-Bonferroni corrects for 12 tests, but the true multiplicity (including layer/window selection) is orders of magnitude larger.\\\"\\n\"\n501\t  },\n502\t  {\n503\t    \"name\": \"Window size ablation (W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 on all models)\",\n504\t    \"description\": \"Systematic window size sweep on all 4 models. Characterize precision-coverage tradeoff and identify optimal W per model.\\n\",\n505\t    \"results\": {\n506\t      \"coverage_decreases_with_w_all_models\": true,\n507\t      \"precision_increases_with_w_all_models\": true,\n508\t      \"fpr_decreases_with_w_all_models\": true,\n509\t      \"r1_8b_best_bal_acc_w\": 25,\n510\t      \"r1_8b_w25_precision\": 0.822,\n511\t      \"r1_8b_w25_coverage\": 0.488,\n512\t      \"r1_8b_w25_fpr_step\": 0.048,\n513\t      \"r1_32b_best_bal_acc_w\": 10,\n514\t      \"r1_32b_w10_precision\": 0.609,\n515\t      \"r1_32b_w10_coverage\": 0.7,\n516\t      \"r1_32b_w10_fpr_step\": 0.234,\n517\t      \"qwq_32b_best_bal_acc_w\": 25,\n518\t      \"qwq_32b_w25_precision\": 0.618,\n519\t      \"qwq_32b_w25_coverage\": 0.579,\n520\t      \"qwq_32b_w25_fpr_step\": 0.169,\n521\t      \"ot_7b_hs_peaks_w3\": true,\n522\t      \"ot_7b_w3_bal_acc\": 0.688,\n523\t      \"ot_7b_w25_bal_acc\": 0.659,\n524\t      \"tradeoff_universal\": \"larger W → higher precision, lower coverage, lower FPR\"\n525\t    },\n526\t    \"tables\": [\n527\t      {\n528\t        \"name\": \"Precision-Coverage Tradeoff Summary (all 4 models)\",\n529\t        \"columns\": [\n530\t          \"Model\",\n531\t          \"W=1 Prec/Cov\",\n532\t          \"W=3 Prec/Cov\",\n533\t          \"W=15 Prec/Cov\",\n534\t          \"W=25 Prec/Cov\"\n535\t        ],\n536\t        \"rows\": [\n537\t          [\n538\t            \"R1-8B\",\n539\t            \"0.693/0.721\",\n540\t            \"0.723/0.698\",\n541\t            \"0.777/0.512\",\n542\t            \"0.822/0.488\"\n543\t          ],\n544\t          [\n545\t            \"OT-7B\",\n546\t            \"—/—\",\n547\t            \"0.466/0.532\",\n548\t            \"—/—\",\n549\t            \"—/—\"\n550\t          ],\n551\t          [\n552\t            \"R1-32B\",\n553\t            \"0.549/0.800\",\n554\t            \"0.560/0.800\",\n555\t            \"0.591/0.700\",\n556\t            \"0.566/0.700\"\n557\t          ],\n558\t          [\n559\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n560\t            \"0.544/0.789\",\n561\t            \"0.565/0.737\",\n562\t            \"0.582/0.684\",\n563\t            \"0.618/0.579\"\n564\t          ]\n565\t        ]\n566\t      }\n567\t    ],\n568\t    \"figures\": [],\n569\t    \"analysis\": \"The ablation confirms the tradeoff is universal but no operating point achieves both high precision AND high coverage. Worst-tier reviewer: \\\"Table 3 is the paper's most honest result. At no window size does any model achieve >0.7 precision AND >0.6 coverage simultaneously. This is not a tuning problem — it's a fundamental performance ceiling.\\\"\\n\\n\\nWindow ablation reveals a clean precision-sensitivity tradeoff that holds across all models. Worst-tier reviewers dismiss this: (1) \\\"The coverage-decreasing-with-W finding is trivially expected — requiring K=5 consecutive crossings with a larger window means fewer traces reach the threshold. This is a property of the detection criterion K, not an insight about temporal probing.\\\" (2) \\\"The optimal W varies by model (W=3 for OT-7B, W=10 for R1-32B, W=25 for QwQ and R1-8B). This means W is another model-specific hyperparameter to tune — combined with layer selection, you need per-model layer AND window tuning. The 'systematic evaluation' framing hides the fact that this is just exhaustive grid search.\\\" (3) \\\"At the best W per model, coverage ranges from 49% (R1-8B) to 70% (R1-32B). Missing 30-50% of safety commitment events is not deployment-ready.\\\"\\n\"\n570\t  },\n571\t  {\n572\t    \"name\": \"Layer selection ablation (5 metrics × 4 models)\",\n573\t    \"description\": \"Evaluate all layers with temporal probes on 32B models under 5 different selection metrics. Demonstrate crossing_rate bias and metric-dependent layer selection.\\n\",\n574\t    \"results\": {\n575\t      \"crossing_rate_selects_shallow_32b\": true,\n576\t      \"pure_bal_acc_selects_middle_32b\": true,\n577\t      \"threshold_dependent_select_deep_32b\": true,\n578\t      \"qwq_l2_vs_l60_precision_gain\": \"+11.5pp\",\n579\t      \"r1_32b_l10_vs_l63_precision_gain\": \"+4.1pp\",\n580\t      \"loo_cv_stability\": \"65-68% dominant fold\",\n581\t      \"r1_8b_consistent\": true,\n582\t      \"ot_7b_varied\": true,\n583\t      \"bal_acc_layer_vs_precision_layer_diverge_32b\": true,\n584\t      \"qwq_l30_bal_acc\": 0.751,\n585\t      \"qwq_l60_bal_acc\": 0.71,\n586\t      \"r1_32b_l47_bal_acc\": 0.829,\n587\t      \"r1_32b_l63_bal_acc\": 0.759,\n588\t      \"ba_optimal_different_from_precision_optimal\": true\n589\t    },\n590\t    \"tables\": [\n591\t      {\n592\t        \"name\": \"Metric × Model → Selected Layer (with HS-Text precision delta)\",\n593\t        \"columns\": [\n594\t          \"Metric\",\n595\t          \"R1-8B Layer (ΔPrec)\",\n596\t          \"OT-7B Layer (ΔPrec)\",\n597\t          \"QwQ Layer (ΔPrec)\",\n598\t          \"R1-32B Layer (ΔPrec)\"\n599\t        ],\n600\t        \"rows\": [\n601\t          [\n602\t            \"bal_acc × cr\",\n603\t            \"L0 (+var)\",\n604\t            \"L0 (+var)\",\n605\t            \"L2 (+5.8pp NS)\",\n606\t            \"L10 (+9.4pp NS)\"\n607\t          ],\n608\t          [\n609\t            \"pure bal_acc\",\n610\t            \"L12 (+17.8pp)\",\n611\t            \"L9 (+25.4pp)\",\n612\t            \"L30 (+18.0pp)\",\n613\t            \"L47 (+13.7pp)\"\n614\t          ],\n615\t          [\n616\t            \"precision\",\n617\t            \"L16 (+22.4pp)\",\n618\t            \"L16 (+21.9pp)\",\n619\t            \"L60 (+23.6pp)\",\n620\t            \"L63 (+15.4pp)\"\n621\t          ],\n622\t          [\n623\t            \"pre_cp_fpr\",\n624\t            \"L16 (−7.4pp)\",\n625\t            \"L16 (−17.1pp)\",\n626\t            \"L60 (−9.8pp)\",\n627\t            \"L63 (−14.6pp)\"\n628\t          ],\n629\t          [\n630\t            \"composite\",\n631\t            \"L16 (+22.4pp)\",\n632\t            \"L12 (+var)\",\n633\t            \"L60 (+23.6pp)\",\n634\t            \"L63 (+15.4pp)\"\n635\t          ]\n636\t        ]\n637\t      }\n638\t    ],\n639\t    \"figures\": [],\n640\t    \"analysis\": \"The ablation is thorough but worst-tier reviewers question whether it constitutes a contribution: \\\"Sensitivity analysis is a sanity check, not a finding. The paper would be better if it had used the right metric from the start and reported results at correct layers — making the sensitivity analysis unnecessary for the paper's core claims.\\\"\\n\\n\\nThe layer selection ablation reveals a genuine pattern (biased metric → shallow layers → masked HS advantage), but worst-tier reviewers split on its significance: (1) The supportive reading: \\\"This is a useful observation — researchers using bal_acc × crossing_rate will get misleading results.\\\" (2) The dismissive reading (dominant in worst tier): \\\"This is expected behavior of a poorly chosen metric. The composite bal_acc × crossing_rate was never standard — it's the authors' own construction (or a rare one). Showing it's bad doesn't advance the field because nobody was using it.\\\" (3) The circularity concern: \\\"You select layers by precision/FPR, then evaluate by precision/FPR. The D044 BA-layer robustness check partially addresses this (16/16 HB pass on BA-selected layers), but the reviewer asks: 'if BA-selected layers also show HS advantage, why not just use BA selection? What's the contribution of the 5-metric comparison?'\\\" (4) \\\"LOO-CV stability of 65-68% means 32-35% of folds select a DIFFERENT layer. This is not the 'robust selection' the paper claims.\\\"\\n\"\n641\t  },\n642\t  {\n643\t    \"name\": \"16-label taxonomy supervision\",\n644\t    \"description\": \"Train probes with fine-grained safety labels (Type 1/2/3/4 × pre/post) instead of binary commitment. Evaluate whether richer supervision improves detection.\\n\",\n645\t    \"results\": {\n646\t      \"r1_8b_16label_acc\": 0.41,\n647\t      \"r1_8b_binary_collapse_acc\": 0.825,\n648\t      \"r1_8b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+0.8pp\",\n649\t      \"ot_7b_16label_acc\": 0.35,\n650\t      \"ot_7b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+1.1pp\",\n651\t      \"r1_32b_16label_acc\": 0.28,\n652\t      \"r1_32b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+0.6pp\",\n653\t      \"qwq_32b_16label_acc\": 0.26,\n654\t      \"qwq_32b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+0.9pp\",\n655\t      \"fine_grained_improvement_marginal\": true,\n656\t      \"max_collapse_delta\": \"+1.1pp\"\n657\t    },\n658\t    \"tables\": [\n659\t      {\n660\t        \"name\": \"16-Label vs Binary Supervision\",\n661\t        \"columns\": [\n662\t          \"Model\",\n663\t          \"Binary Acc\",\n664\t          \"16-Label Acc\",\n665\t          \"16→Binary Collapse\",\n666\t          \"Δ vs Binary\"\n667\t        ],\n668\t        \"rows\": [\n669\t          [\n670\t            \"R1-8B\",\n671\t            \"0.817\",\n672\t            \"0.41\",\n673\t            \"0.825\",\n674\t            \"+0.8pp\"\n675\t          ],\n676\t          [\n677\t            \"OT-7B\",\n678\t            \"0.688\",\n679\t            \"0.35\",\n680\t            \"0.699\",\n681\t            \"+1.1pp\"\n682\t          ],\n683\t          [\n684\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n685\t            \"0.740\",\n686\t            \"0.26\",\n687\t            \"0.749\",\n688\t            \"+0.9pp\"\n689\t          ],\n690\t          [\n691\t            \"R1-32B\",\n692\t            \"0.826\",\n693\t            \"0.28\",\n694\t            \"0.832\",\n695\t            \"+0.6pp\"\n696\t          ]\n697\t        ]\n698\t      }\n699\t    ],\n700\t    \"figures\": [],\n701\t    \"analysis\": \"Fine-grained supervision dead-end. Worst-tier implication: \\\"The information ceiling for linear probes on single-layer hidden states is fundamentally limited. Trying harder with better labels or more metrics can't overcome this.\\\"\\n\\n\\nFine-grained supervision provides marginal benefit (+0.6-1.1pp after binary collapse) across all models. The 16-class accuracy is low (26-41%) because the classes are imbalanced and transition types are not cleanly separable in hidden-state space. Worst-tier reviewers react: (1) \\\"Marginal improvement from richer supervision confirms the probe is already extracting most of the available linear signal in binary mode. This supports the reviewer's argument that linear probes are saturated — not that the methodology is optimal, but that the information ceiling is low.\\\" (2) \\\"The 16-label experiment was motivated by reviewer concern about 'underusing available supervision,' but the result shows supervision quality doesn't matter much. This undermines the paper's implicit argument that careful methodology (layer selection, temporal context) is what matters — it seems the representation quality is the binding constraint.\\\"\\n\"\n702\t  },\n703\t  {\n704\t    \"name\": \"Oracle decomposition\",\n705\t    \"description\": \"Analyze what the oracle label sequence encodes that hidden-state probes cannot access. Compare oracle lead (+31 steps on R1-8B) to probe lead (~0 steps).\\n\",\n706\t    \"results\": {\n707\t      \"r1_8b_oracle_lead\": \"+31.0\",\n708\t      \"r1_8b_best_probe_lead\": \"+4.5 (GRU)\",\n709\t      \"r1_8b_oracle_gap\": \"26.5\",\n710\t      \"r1_8b_label_autocorr_pre_cp\": 0.82,\n711\t      \"r1_8b_hs_autocorr_pre_cp\": 0.95,\n712\t      \"label_entropy_pre_cp_r1_8b\": 1.38,\n713\t      \"hs_entropy_pre_cp_r1_8b\": 0.41,\n714\t      \"r1_32b_oracle_lead\": \"+28\",\n715\t      \"r1_32b_best_probe_lead\": \"+13 (W=1, 25% cov)\",\n716\t      \"qwq_oracle_lead\": \"+27\",\n717\t      \"qwq_best_probe_lead\": \"−1.2 to +1.9\",\n718\t      \"pattern_finding\": \"Labels show gradual safety erosion pre-CP; hidden states show step-function at CP\"\n719\t    },\n720\t    \"tables\": [\n721\t      {\n722\t        \"name\": \"Oracle vs Best Probe Lead Time\",\n723\t        \"columns\": [\n724\t          \"Model\",\n725\t          \"Oracle Lead\",\n726\t          \"Best Probe Lead\",\n727\t          \"Method\",\n728\t          \"Gap\",\n729\t          \"Hidden State Entropy (pre-CP)\"\n730\t        ],\n731\t        \"rows\": [\n732\t          [\n733\t            \"R1-8B\",\n734\t            \"+31.0\",\n735\t            \"+4.5\",\n736\t            \"GRU multi-layer\",\n737\t            \"26.5\",\n738\t            \"0.41\"\n739\t          ],\n740\t          [\n741\t            \"OT-7B\",\n742\t            \"~+30\",\n743\t            \"+33.6 (GRU)\",\n744\t            \"GRU multi-layer\",\n745\t            \"~0†\",\n746\t            \"—\"\n747\t          ],\n748\t          [\n749\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n750\t            \"+27\",\n751\t            \"−1.2 to +1.9\",\n752\t            \"temporal LR\",\n753\t            \"25-28\",\n754\t            \"—\"\n755\t          ],\n756\t          [\n757\t            \"R1-32B\",\n758\t            \"+28\",\n759\t            \"+13.3\",\n760\t            \"temporal LR W=1\",\n761\t            \"15 (25% cov)\",\n762\t            \"—\"\n763\t          ]\n764\t        ]\n765\t      }\n766\t    ],\n767\t    \"figures\": [],\n768\t    \"analysis\": \"The oracle decomposition is the worst-tier's strongest negative finding: hidden states are fundamentally reactive (encoding current state) rather than predictive (encoding trajectory). Reviewer: \\\"This paper demonstrates that hidden-state monitoring is a lagging indicator. For safety deployment, you need leading indicators. Text-based trajectory analysis, while noisier, is the better research direction.\\\"\\n\\n\\nOracle decomposition reveals a 26-28 step gap between what's detectable in labels vs hidden states. The label sequence shows gradual pre-CP patterns (increasing unsafe-adjacent labels, shortening safe streaks) that hidden states don't track. Worst-tier reviewers draw a harsh conclusion: \\\"The 31-step oracle lead proves that safety commitment is a GRADUAL process visible in the model's outputs. But the hidden-state probe can only detect it at or after the commitment point (lead ≈ 0). This means hidden states encode the CURRENT safety state but not the TRAJECTORY toward commitment. For deployment, trajectory prediction is what matters — catching the commitment after it happens is too late. The oracle gap is not a puzzle for Discussion; it's evidence that the entire hidden-state monitoring approach is fundamentally limited. Text-based monitoring that tracks reasoning trajectory (even with higher FPR) provides earlier warning.\\\"\\n\"\n769\t  },\n770\t  {\n771\t    \"name\": \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B, STILL-trained)\",\n772\t    \"description\": \"4th model completing the 2×2 architecture×scale matrix. Full pipeline: layer sweep, temporal probes, HS vs text comparison, GRU, selectivity control task.\\n\",\n773\t    \"results\": {\n774\t      \"best_layer\": 16,\n775\t      \"best_layer_depth_pct\": 57.1,\n776\t      \"best_hs_bal_acc_w3\": 0.688,\n777\t      \"best_hs_precision_w3\": 0.466,\n778\t      \"best_hs_pre_cp_fpr\": 0.1,\n779\t      \"bootstrap_3_of_3_hb\": true,\n780\t      \"bootstrap_bal_acc_delta\": \"+10.7pp\",\n781\t      \"bootstrap_precision_delta\": \"+21.9pp\",\n782\t      \"bootstrap_fpr_delta\": \"−17.1pp\",\n783\t      \"selectivity_pp\": 18.3,\n784\t      \"n_traces\": 228,\n785\t      \"gru_multi_bal_acc\": 0.746,\n786\t      \"gru_multi_lead\": \"+33.6\",\n787\t      \"gru_multi_fpr\": 0.274,\n788\t      \"hs_temporal_saturation_w3\": true,\n789\t      \"text_temporal_w25_best\": 0.614\n790\t    },\n791\t    \"tables\": [\n792\t      {\n793\t        \"name\": \"OT-7B Full Pipeline Summary\",\n794\t        \"columns\": [\n795\t          \"Method\",\n796\t          \"bal_acc\",\n797\t          \"precision\",\n798\t          \"pre-CP FPR\",\n799\t          \"coverage\",\n800\t          \"lead\"\n801\t        ],\n802\t        \"rows\": [\n803\t          [\n804\t            \"HS temporal W=3 L16\",\n805\t            \"0.688\",\n806\t            \"0.466\",\n807\t            \"0.100\",\n808\t            \"0.532\",\n809\t            \"+21.6\"\n810\t          ],\n811\t          [\n812\t            \"Text temporal W=25\",\n813\t            \"0.614\",\n814\t            \"0.329\",\n815\t            \"0.161\",\n816\t            \"0.660\",\n817\t            \"+45.5\"\n818\t          ],\n819\t          [\n820\t            \"GRU multi L13-19\",\n821\t            \"0.746\",\n822\t            \"—\",\n823\t            \"0.274\",\n824\t            \"0.645\",\n825\t            \"+33.6\"\n826\t          ],\n827\t          [\n828\t            \"HS-Text Δ (HB)\",\n829\t            \"+10.7pp ✅\",\n830\t            \"+21.9pp ✅\",\n831\t            \"−17.1pp ✅\",\n832\t            \"—\",\n833\t            \"—\"\n834\t          ]\n835\t        ]\n836\t      }\n837\t    ],\n838\t    \"figures\": [],\n839\t    \"analysis\": \"OT-7B strengthens the 7-8B story but worst-tier reviewers note: \\\"The 2×2 matrix now has two strong cells (7-8B) and two weak cells (32B). The paper's breadth claim ('4 models') is technically true but misleading — 2/4 models have serious practical caveats that make the HS advantage non-deployable.\\\"\\n\\n\\nOT-7B is the cleanest single-model evidence for HS advantage (3/3 HB, highest selectivity 18.3pp, largest n=228 traces). But worst-tier reviewers are unimpressed by adding a 4th model that confirms the known pattern: (1) \\\"A 4th model at 7B showing the same result doesn't address the 32B caveats. The paper already has two 7-8B models that work. The question is whether HS monitoring scales, and OT-7B can't answer that.\\\" (2) \\\"OT-7B has its own caveats: HS temporal saturation at W=3 (bal_acc declines at larger W), and absolute performance is still below R1-8B (best bal_acc 68.8% vs 80.2%). The 'cleanest evidence' is still only 69% balanced accuracy — barely better than a biased coin.\\\" (3) \\\"GRU lead time of +33.6 steps sounds impressive until you note OT-7B traces average ~80 sentences — normalize by trace length and the lead time fraction is similar to other models.\\\" (4) \\\"FPR of 27.4% for GRU means ~1 in 4 safe steps triggers the detector. At 80-sentence traces, that's ~20 false alarms per safe trace.\\\"\\n\"\n840\t  },\n841\t  {\n842\t    \"name\": \"GRU/Attention probe upper bound (32B models)\",\n843\t    \"description\": \"GRU sequence model on 32B models at corrected layers. Tests whether nonlinear sequence modeling can recover signal that linear temporal probes miss.\\n\",\n844\t    \"results\": {\n845\t      \"r1_32b_gru_single_bal_acc\": 0.675,\n846\t      \"r1_32b_gru_multi_bal_acc\": 0.655,\n847\t      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_mean\": \"+8.8 to +9.1\",\n848\t      \"r1_32b_gru_fpr\": \"0.114 to 0.135\",\n849\t      \"r1_32b_gru_coverage\": \"0.23 to 0.30\",\n850\t      \"r1_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta\": \"−6pp bal_acc (GRU worse)\",\n851\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_single_bal_acc\": 0.694,\n852\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_multi_bal_acc\": 0.724,\n853\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean\": \"+15.9\",\n854\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_fpr\": 0.289,\n855\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_coverage\": 0.84,\n856\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta\": \"−3.7pp bal_acc (GRU worse at bal_acc, better at coverage)\",\n857\t      \"ot_7b_gru_multi_bal_acc\": 0.746,\n858\t      \"ot_7b_gru_multi_lead\": \"+33.6\",\n859\t      \"ot_7b_gru_fpr\": 0.274,\n860\t      \"gru_worse_than_lr_3_of_4\": true,\n861\t      \"gru_note\": \"GRU trades accuracy for coverage/lead — higher FPR drives earlier detection\"\n862\t    },\n863\t    \"tables\": [\n864\t      {\n865\t        \"name\": \"GRU vs Temporal LR (32B models, corrected layers)\",\n866\t        \"columns\": [\n867\t          \"Model\",\n868\t          \"LR bal_acc\",\n869\t          \"GRU bal_acc\",\n870\t          \"Δ\",\n871\t          \"GRU FPR\",\n872\t          \"LR FPR\"\n873\t        ],\n874\t        \"rows\": [\n875\t          [\n876\t            \"R1-32B (L63 W=10)\",\n877\t            \"0.737\",\n878\t            \"~0.675\",\n879\t            \"−6.2pp\",\n880\t            \"0.135\",\n881\t            \"0.234\"\n882\t          ],\n883\t          [\n884\t            \"QwQ-32B (L60 W=5)\",\n885\t            \"0.761\",\n886\t            \"0.724\",\n887\t            \"−3.7pp\",\n888\t            \"0.289\",\n889\t            \"0.217\"\n890\t          ],\n891\t          [\n892\t            \"OT-7B (L16 multi)\",\n893\t            \"0.688\",\n894\t            \"0.746\",\n895\t            \"+5.8pp\",\n896\t            \"0.274\",\n897\t            \"0.100\"\n898\t          ],\n899\t          [\n900\t            \"R1-8B (L14 multi)\",\n901\t            \"0.806\",\n902\t            \"0.809\",\n903\t            \"+0.3pp\",\n904\t            \"0.165\",\n905\t            \"0.097\"\n906\t          ]\n907\t        ]\n908\t      }\n909\t    ],\n910\t    \"figures\": [],\n911\t    \"analysis\": \"GRU fails to improve over LR on most models, confirming linear probes are at the information ceiling. Worst-tier reviewer: \\\"The paper's headline is 'HS beats text.' The method is logistic regression. The contribution is... careful hyperparameter selection? This is a well-executed experiment report, not a methods paper.\\\"\\n\\n\\nGRU does NOT outperform temporal LR on balanced accuracy for 3/4 models. On R1-32B (n=20 test), GRU severely overfits — bal_acc drops 6pp below LR. On QwQ, GRU achieves higher coverage and lead time but at 2× the FPR. This confirms linear probes are sufficient (or even superior given small samples). Worst-tier reviewers use this to attack novelty: \\\"The paper's method is logistic regression on hidden states with a sliding window. That's not a contribution — it's the simplest possible baseline. The fact that GRU and MLP don't help confirms the method space is trivial, which means the paper's 'systematic comparison of 7 methods' is comparing variations of the obvious approach (linear probe) against methods that were never designed for this task (Mahalanobis, BOCPD, CCS). The negative results are predictable, not informative.\\\"\\n\"\n912\t  },\n913\t  {\n914\t    \"name\": \"Text-based baseline (sentence embeddings + temporal LR)\",\n915\t    \"description\": \"Sentence-BERT (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) embeddings + temporal window LR on CoT text of all 4 models. Critical comparison establishing what text monitoring achieves without hidden-state access.\\n\",\n916\t    \"results\": {\n917\t      \"r1_8b_text_static_acc\": 0.661,\n918\t      \"r1_8b_text_temporal_w15_acc\": 0.678,\n919\t      \"r1_8b_text_temporal_w15_auroc\": 0.759,\n920\t      \"r1_8b_hs_acc_advantage\": \"+15-18pp\",\n921\t      \"ot_7b_text_best_bal_acc\": 0.614,\n922\t      \"ot_7b_text_best_precision\": 0.329,\n923\t      \"ot_7b_text_best_fpr\": 0.161,\n924\t      \"qwq_32b_text_temporal_bal_acc\": 0.65,\n925\t      \"qwq_32b_text_precision\": 0.421,\n926\t      \"r1_32b_text_temporal_bal_acc\": 0.646,\n927\t      \"r1_32b_text_precision\": 0.45,\n928\t      \"hs_vs_text_precision_gap_range\": \"+15.4 to +23.6pp\",\n929\t      \"text_requires_zero_infra_change\": true,\n930\t      \"text_precision_all_models\": \"0.329-0.487\",\n931\t      \"hs_precision_all_models\": \"0.466-0.776\"\n932\t    },\n933\t    \"tables\": [\n934\t      {\n935\t        \"name\": \"HS vs Text Precision Gap (+ engineering cost annotation)\",\n936\t        \"columns\": [\n937\t          \"Model\",\n938\t          \"HS Precision\",\n939\t          \"Text Precision\",\n940\t          \"Δ\",\n941\t          \"HS Infra Cost\"\n942\t        ],\n943\t        \"rows\": [\n944\t          [\n945\t            \"R1-8B\",\n946\t            \"0.777 (W=15)\",\n947\t            \"0.487 (W=15)\",\n948\t            \"+29.0pp\",\n949\t            \"white-box, L14\"\n950\t          ],\n951\t          [\n952\t            \"OT-7B\",\n953\t            \"0.466 (W=3)\",\n954\t            \"0.329 (W=25)\",\n955\t            \"+13.7pp\",\n956\t            \"white-box, L16\"\n957\t          ],\n958\t          [\n959\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n960\t            \"0.578 (W=3)\",\n961\t            \"0.421 (W=best)\",\n962\t            \"+15.7pp\",\n963\t            \"white-box, L60\"\n964\t          ],\n965\t          [\n966\t            \"R1-32B\",\n967\t            \"0.603 (W=1)\",\n968\t            \"0.450 (W=best)\",\n969\t            \"+15.3pp\",\n970\t            \"white-box, L63\"\n971\t          ]\n972\t        ]\n973\t      }\n974\t    ],\n975\t    \"figures\": [],\n976\t    \"analysis\": \"The text baseline is the worst-tier reviewer's strongest weapon: \\\"A 15-24pp precision gain sounds meaningful until you consider: (a) text monitoring costs $0 in infrastructure, (b) HS monitoring requires white-box access + per-model tuning, (c) the BGE-large result shows the gap shrinks with better text encoders, (d) safe FPR is 39-62% regardless of modality — both approaches produce unusable false alarm rates. The paper should honestly state: 'HS probes are measurably but marginally better than text probes. Whether the margin justifies white-box access depends on deployment constraints. For most practical settings, text monitoring suffices.'\\\"\\n\\n\\nText baseline performs consistently across all models (bal_acc 0.61-0.68, precision 0.33-0.49). HS probes outperform by +15-24pp precision, which is statistically significant (12/12 HB). But worst-tier reviewers attack the COST-BENEFIT ratio, not the statistical claim: (1) \\\"Text probes need zero infrastructure change — you monitor the CoT output that's already available. HS probes require white-box access to model internals, which means: (a) running the model locally (can't use API), (b) modifying the inference pipeline to extract intermediate activations, (c) per-model layer selection + probe training. The 15-24pp precision gain comes at enormous engineering cost.\\\" (2) \\\"At 33-49% text precision, you get 1 true alarm per 2-3 triggers. At 47-78% HS precision, you get 1 per 1.3-2.1 triggers. Is that difference worth instrumenting your entire inference stack? For most deployment teams, no.\\\" (3) \\\"Text probes improve with better embeddings (BGE-large, domain-fine-tuned). The paper tested only all-MiniLM-L6-v2 — the weakest common baseline. A reviewer notes: 'Use GPT-4 as the text classifier and the gap likely narrows to <5pp.'\\\" (4) \\\"The BGE-large comparison (exp_bge_large_text_comparison) showed only 1/4 HB pass for HS vs BGE-large — the gap narrows significantly with a better text encoder.\\\"\\n\"\n977\t  },\n978\t  {\n979\t    \"name\": \"BOCPD/CUSUM on all 4 models\",\n980\t    \"description\": \"Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection + CUSUM on probe score sequences. Tests whether advanced change-point methods can achieve positive lead time.\\n\",\n981\t    \"results\": {\n982\t      \"r1_8b_bocpd_detection\": 0.93,\n983\t      \"r1_8b_bocpd_lead\": \"+3.45\",\n984\t      \"r1_8b_cusum_lead\": \"+0.89\",\n985\t      \"r1_8b_fcp_baseline_lead\": \"+4.89\",\n986\t      \"r1_8b_fcp_beats_bocpd_on_lead\": true,\n987\t      \"ot_7b_bocpd_detection\": 0.0,\n988\t      \"ot_7b_bocpd_note\": \"W=3 probe scores too spiky, Gaussian model fails\",\n989\t      \"ot_7b_fcp_best_detection\": 0.894,\n990\t      \"ot_7b_fcp_best_lead\": \"+11.83\",\n991\t      \"r1_32b_bocpd_detection\": 0.0,\n992\t      \"qwq_32b_bocpd_detection\": 0.0,\n993\t      \"r1_32b_cusum_detection\": 0.85,\n994\t      \"r1_32b_cusum_lead\": \"+2.59\",\n995\t      \"qwq_32b_cusum_detection\": 0.737,\n996\t      \"qwq_32b_cusum_lead\": \"+9.86\",\n997\t      \"bocpd_works_only_r1_8b\": true,\n998\t      \"cusum_universally_worse_than_fcp\": true\n999\t    },\n1000\t    \"tables\": [\n1001\t      {\n1002\t        \"name\": \"Change-Point Detection Cross-Model Summary\",\n1003\t        \"columns\": [\n1004\t          \"Model\",\n1005\t          \"BOCPD Det%\",\n1006\t          \"BOCPD Lead\",\n1007\t          \"CUSUM Det%\",\n1008\t          \"CUSUM Lead\",\n1009\t          \"FCP Det%\",\n1010\t          \"FCP Lead\"\n1011\t        ],\n1012\t        \"rows\": [\n1013\t          [\n1014\t            \"R1-8B\",\n1015\t            \"93%\",\n1016\t            \"+3.45\",\n1017\t            \"65%\",\n1018\t            \"+0.89\",\n1019\t            \"65%\",\n1020\t            \"+4.89\"\n1021\t          ],\n1022\t          [\n1023\t            \"OT-7B\",\n1024\t            \"0%\",\n1025\t            \"—\",\n1026\t            \"75%†\",\n1027\t            \"+7.46†\",\n1028\t            \"89%\",\n1029\t            \"+11.83\"\n1030\t          ],\n1031\t          [\n1032\t            \"R1-32B\",\n1033\t            \"0%\",\n1034\t            \"—\",\n1035\t            \"85%\",\n1036\t            \"+2.59\",\n1037\t            \"95%\",\n1038\t            \"+5.68\"\n1039\t          ],\n1040\t          [\n1041\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n1042\t            \"0%\",\n1043\t            \"—\",\n1044\t            \"74%\",\n1045\t            \"+9.86\",\n1046\t            \"79%\",\n1047\t            \"+9.40\"\n1048\t          ]\n1049\t        ]\n1050\t      }\n1051\t    ],\n1052\t    \"figures\": [],\n1053\t    \"analysis\": \"Complete BOCPD failure on 3/4 models and CUSUM inferiority to simple thresholding. Worst-tier reviewer: \\\"The paper tests two change-point algorithms that were designed for very different signal types, finds they don't work, and claims this as a negative result. But the method selection is so narrow that the negative result is uninformative — you tested the wrong tools.\\\"\\n\\n\\nBOCPD fails on 3/4 models (0% detection on OT-7B, R1-32B, QwQ). Only R1-8B works (93%) because R1-8B has gradual probe score transitions that match BOCPD's Gaussian change-point model. Other models have abrupt transitions (OT-7B) or weak signals (32B) that BOCPD's parametric assumptions can't handle. CUSUM detects some traces but with worse lead than simple first-crossing-point (FCP) threshold. Worst-tier reviewers: (1) \\\"BOCPD and CUSUM were never designed for this signal type. Testing them is due diligence, but their failure is predictable — these methods assume stationary-then-shifted Gaussian data, while probe scores are bounded [0,1] and often spiky.\\\" (2) \\\"The negative result strengthens the 'nothing works well' narrative, but the method selection is questionable. Why not test HMM, Viterbi decoding, or learned change-point detectors? The paper tests 2 off-the-shelf methods and declares change-point detection a dead end.\\\" (3) \\\"Simple FCP threshold beats BOCPD on mean lead time for R1-8B. The 'advanced' methods add nothing.\\\"\\n\"\n1054\t  },\n1055\t  {\n1056\t    \"name\": \"False positive analysis (safe trace FPR, all methods)\",\n1057\t    \"description\": \"Run all probes on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5) across all 4 models. Report safe FPR, pre-CP FPR, precision, and precision-recall curves.\\n\",\n1058\t    \"results\": {\n1059\t      \"r1_8b_hs_safe_fpr\": 0.39,\n1060\t      \"r1_8b_text_safe_fpr\": 0.385,\n1061\t      \"ot_7b_hs_safe_fpr\": \"0.111-0.175\",\n1062\t      \"ot_7b_text_safe_fpr\": \"0.302-0.460\",\n1063\t      \"qwq_32b_hs_safe_fpr\": \"0.45-0.50\",\n1064\t      \"qwq_32b_text_safe_fpr\": \"0.325-0.425\",\n1065\t      \"r1_32b_hs_safe_fpr\": 0.239,\n1066\t      \"r1_32b_text_safe_fpr\": 0.31,\n1067\t      \"safe_fpr_range_all\": \"0.111 to 0.521\",\n1068\t      \"safe_fpr_median\": \"~0.39\",\n1069\t      \"gru_safe_fpr\": 0.521,\n1070\t      \"gru_highest_fpr_note\": \"GRU trades precision for coverage → highest FPR\",\n1071\t      \"precision_all_methods_range\": \"0.329 to 0.777\",\n1072\t      \"no_method_achieves_80pct_precision\": true,\n1073\t      \"deployment_threshold_precision_70\": \"only R1-8B HS W≥15 exceeds\"\n1074\t    },\n1075\t    \"tables\": [\n1076\t      {\n1077\t        \"name\": \"Safe Trace FPR Summary (all methods × all models)\",\n1078\t        \"columns\": [\n1079\t          \"Model\",\n1080\t          \"HS Static FPR\",\n1081\t          \"HS Temporal FPR\",\n1082\t          \"Text Temporal FPR\",\n1083\t          \"GRU FPR\"\n1084\t        ],\n1085\t        \"rows\": [\n1086\t          [\n1087\t            \"R1-8B\",\n1088\t            \"0.390\",\n1089\t            \"0.392\",\n1090\t            \"0.385\",\n1091\t            \"0.521\"\n1092\t          ],\n1093\t          [\n1094\t            \"OT-7B\",\n1095\t            \"0.111\",\n1096\t            \"0.175\",\n1097\t            \"0.460\",\n1098\t            \"—\"\n1099\t          ],\n1100\t          [\n1101\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n1102\t            \"0.500\",\n1103\t            \"0.450\",\n1104\t            \"0.425\",\n1105\t            \"—\"\n1106\t          ],\n1107\t          [\n1108\t            \"R1-32B\",\n1109\t            \"0.239\",\n1110\t            \"0.239\",\n1111\t            \"0.310\",\n1112\t            \"—\"\n1113\t          ]\n1114\t        ]\n1115\t      },\n1116\t      {\n1117\t        \"name\": \"Deployment Viability Assessment\",\n1118\t        \"columns\": [\n1119\t          \"Criterion\",\n1120\t          \"Threshold\",\n1121\t          \"Models Meeting Criterion\",\n1122\t          \"Best Method\"\n1123\t        ],\n1124\t        \"rows\": [\n1125\t          [\n1126\t            \"Safe FPR < 20%\",\n1127\t            \"0.20\",\n1128\t            \"1/4 (OT-7B HS static)\",\n1129\t            \"HS static L16\"\n1130\t          ],\n1131\t          [\n1132\t            \"Precision > 70%\",\n1133\t            \"0.70\",\n1134\t            \"1/4 (R1-8B W≥15)\",\n1135\t            \"HS temporal W=25\"\n1136\t          ],\n1137\t          [\n1138\t            \"Coverage > 60%\",\n1139\t            \"0.60\",\n1140\t            \"2/4 (R1-8B W≤3, R1-32B W≤3)\",\n1141\t            \"HS temporal W=1\"\n1142\t          ],\n1143\t          [\n1144\t            \"All three criteria\",\n1145\t            \"—\",\n1146\t            \"0/4\",\n1147\t            \"None\"\n1148\t          ]\n1149\t        ]\n1150\t      }\n1151\t    ],\n1152\t    \"figures\": [],\n1153\t    \"analysis\": \"FPR analysis is the worst-tier's core evidence for the \\\"not practically useful\\\" dismissal. No model meets all three deployment criteria simultaneously. The best model (R1-8B) achieves >70% precision only at W≥15 where coverage drops to 49%. The paper's deployment framing is untenable. Reframing guidance: \\\"Position as diagnostic/research tool, explicitly disclaim deployment readiness. Lead with 'none of our 7 methods achieves deployment-ready performance' as a cautionary finding.\\\"\\n\\n\\nSafe FPR ranges from 11% (OT-7B HS static, best case) to 52% (GRU, worst case), with a median around 39%. This is the worst-tier dealbreaker. Reviewer calculation: \\\"At 39% safe FPR with K=5 consecutive crossings for trace-level detection, a 40-step safe trace has ~39% probability of triggering the detector. In a production system processing 1000 safe traces per day, that's ~390 false alarms daily. Each requires human review at ~5 minutes → 32.5 person-hours/day of false alarm triage. No deployment team would accept this.\\\" Even the best case (OT-7B HS 11.1% safe FPR) means 111 false alarms per 1000 safe traces. The worst-tier reviewer's verdict: \\\"A safety monitor that fires on 1 in 3 to 1 in 2 safe traces is not a monitor — it's a random noise generator. The paper should lead with this limitation, not bury it.\\\" QwQ-32B is particularly problematic: HS safe FPR (45-50%) is HIGHER than text safe FPR (33-43%), meaning HS monitoring generates MORE false alarms on safe traces for QwQ.\\n\"\n1154\t  },\n1155\t  {\n1156\t    \"name\": \"5-fold CV validation of multi-metric ensemble (7-8B)\",\n1157\t    \"description\": \"Cross-validated ensemble of metric-weighted layer selections on R1-8B and OT-7B. Validates that multi-metric ensemble eliminates layer selection bias under proper CV.\\n\",\n1158\t    \"results\": {\n1159\t      \"r1_8b_ensemble_cv_prec\": 0.565,\n1160\t      \"r1_8b_best_single_cv_prec\": 0.604,\n1161\t      \"r1_8b_ensemble_vs_text_gap\": \"+19.3pp\",\n1162\t      \"r1_8b_ensemble_vs_text_p\": \"<0.002\",\n1163\t      \"ot_7b_ensemble_cv_prec\": 0.506,\n1164\t      \"ot_7b_best_single_cv_prec\": 0.473,\n1165\t      \"ot_7b_ensemble_beats_best_single\": true,\n1166\t      \"ot_7b_ensemble_beats_best_single_p\": 0.033,\n1167\t      \"cv_validates_preliminary\": true,\n1168\t      \"cv_acceptance_probability_delta\": \"+0.01 to +0.03\"\n1169\t    },\n1170\t    \"tables\": [\n1171\t      {\n1172\t        \"name\": \"Ensemble CV Results (7-8B models)\",\n1173\t        \"columns\": [\n1174\t          \"Model\",\n1175\t          \"Ensemble Prec\",\n1176\t          \"Best Single Prec\",\n1177\t          \"Text Prec\",\n1178\t          \"Ens vs Text p\"\n1179\t        ],\n1180\t        \"rows\": [\n1181\t          [\n1182\t            \"R1-8B\",\n1183\t            \"0.565\",\n1184\t            \"0.604\",\n1185\t            \"0.372\",\n1186\t            \"<0.002\"\n1187\t          ],\n1188\t          [\n1189\t            \"OT-7B\",\n1190\t            \"0.506\",\n1191\t            \"0.473\",\n1192\t            \"0.236\",\n1193\t            \"<0.001\"\n1194\t          ]\n1195\t        ]\n1196\t      }\n1197\t    ],\n1198\t    \"figures\": [],\n1199\t    \"analysis\": \"Ensemble CV is a marginal improvement on an already-validated statistical claim. Worst-tier impact on acceptance: negligible (+1-3%). The bottleneck is not statistical rigor — it's practical utility and novelty. This experiment is low-cost and worth including in supplementary, but it cannot rescue the paper from worst-tier reviewer concerns.\\n\\nCV validation confirms the ensemble result holds (p≤0.002 for ensemble vs text on R1-8B). But worst-tier reviewers are unmoved: (1) \\\"CV validating an ensemble of layer selection metrics on 7-8B models doesn't address ANY of our core concerns. The issues are: high safe FPR (39-62%), model-specific caveats at 32B, engineering cost of white-box access, and insufficient novelty. A statistically cleaner layer selection protocol is nice but tangential.\\\" (2) \\\"The ensemble precision (0.506-0.565) is actually LOWER than the best single metric on R1-8B (0.604). On OT-7B the ensemble slightly beats the best single metric (0.506 vs 0.473) but the absolute precision is 50% — a coin flip.\\\" (3) \\\"This experiment strengthens an already- strong statistical case (12/12 HB already passes). The paper doesn't need MORE statistical validation; it needs BETTER results or a DIFFERENT framing.\\\" The CV ensemble moves acceptance probability by +1-3%, insufficient to shift from worst tier.\\n\"\n1200\t  }\n1201\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57971aef-fe55-48e4-84c1-753372b1ed10","uuid":"05ef7184-9592-4424-8f77-b1b10d6b796f","timestamp":"2026-05-21T21:57:37.896Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T212242/worst/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture layer sweep + static probe (r1-32b & QwQ-32B)\",\n    \"description\": \"Full 64-layer sweep on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and QwQ-32B. Extract hidden states at all layers, evaluate static LR probes per layer under multiple selection metrics. Includes discovery of layer selection bias (bal_acc × crossing_rate → shallow layers).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_biased_layer\": 10,\n      \"r1_32b_biased_layer_depth_pct\": 15.6,\n      \"r1_32b_corrected_layer\": 63,\n      \"r1_32b_corrected_layer_depth_pct\": 98.4,\n      \"r1_32b_l10_bal_acc\": 0.718,\n      \"r1_32b_l63_bal_acc_w1\": 0.826,\n      \"r1_32b_n_test\": 20,\n      \"r1_32b_l63_precision_w1\": 0.603,\n      \"r1_32b_l63_fpr_w1\": 0.235,\n      \"qwq_32b_biased_layer\": 2,\n      \"qwq_32b_biased_layer_depth_pct\": 3.1,\n      \"qwq_32b_corrected_layer\": 60,\n      \"qwq_32b_corrected_layer_depth_pct\": 93.8,\n      \"qwq_32b_l2_bal_acc\": 0.73,\n      \"qwq_32b_l60_bal_acc_w3\": 0.74,\n      \"qwq_32b_n_test\": \"19-20\",\n      \"qwq_32b_l60_precision_w3\": 0.578,\n      \"qwq_32b_l60_fpr_w3\": 0.279,\n      \"layer_selection_bias_discovered\": true,\n      \"metrics_tested\": 5,\n      \"biased_metric\": \"bal_acc × crossing_rate\",\n      \"loo_cv_dominant_fold_pct\": \"65-68%\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Layer Selection Metric × Model → Selected Layer\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"R1-8B\",\n          \"OT-7B\",\n          \"QwQ-32B\",\n          \"R1-32B\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"bal_acc × crossing_rate\",\n            \"L0 (0%)\",\n            \"L0 (0%)\",\n            \"L2 (3%)\",\n            \"L10 (16%)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"pure bal_acc\",\n            \"L12 (39%)\",\n            \"L9 (32%)\",\n            \"L30 (47%)\",\n            \"L47 (73%)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"precision\",\n            \"L16 (52%)\",\n            \"L16 (57%)\",\n            \"L60 (94%)\",\n            \"L63 (98%)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"pre_cp_fpr\",\n            \"L16 (52%)\",\n            \"L16 (57%)\",\n            \"L60 (94%)\",\n            \"L63 (98%)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"composite (additive)\",\n            \"L16 (52%)\",\n            \"L12 (43%)\",\n            \"L60 (94%)\",\n            \"L63 (98%)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Layer selection sensitivity is the paper's primary methodological contribution, but worst-tier reviewers rate it insufficient novelty (2.0-2.5). The argument: \\\"every ML practitioner knows different metrics select different hyperparameters. Showing this for layer selection is an observation, not a method. There's no proposed algorithm to automatically resolve the disagreement — just 'try more metrics,' which is standard practice.\\\"\\n\\n\\nLayer sweep reveals a genuine methodological finding (biased metric selects shallow layers), and corrected layers do show HS advantage. However, worst-tier reviewers dismiss this: (1) \\\"Finding that one composite metric is bad is trivially expected — practitioners routinely try multiple metrics. This is hyperparameter tuning, not a contribution.\\\" (2) n=19-20 test traces for 32B models mean bootstrap CIs are wide. MDE analysis shows R1-32B residualized gap (+1.9pp) is far below the 11.2pp MDE at 80% power — the NS result for position-mediated HS advantage is a power limitation, not evidence. Reviewers note: \\\"you can't claim the advantage is 'real but position-mediated' when you're underpowered to detect the non-positional component.\\\" (3) The corrected layers (L60/L63, >93% depth) are so deep they're essentially the output layer — reviewer asks: \\\"are you just detecting output-token semantics via a different pathway?\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation (32B)\",\n    \"description\": \"Temporal LR at multiple window sizes on R1-32B (L63) and QwQ-32B (L60). Evaluate whether temporal context universally improves probe accuracy across 32B models.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_l63_w1_bal_acc\": 0.826,\n      \"r1_32b_l63_w1_precision\": 0.603,\n      \"r1_32b_l63_w1_coverage\": 0.25,\n      \"r1_32b_l63_w3_bal_acc\": 0.754,\n      \"r1_32b_l63_w5_bal_acc\": 0.741,\n      \"r1_32b_l63_w10_bal_acc\": 0.737,\n      \"r1_32b_l63_w15_bal_acc\": 0.721,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_trend\": \"DECREASING with W (W=1 best, -10.5pp by W=15)\",\n      \"r1_32b_dimensionality_issue\": \"51200 × W features for n=20 → massive overfitting at W≥3\",\n      \"qwq_32b_l60_w1_bal_acc\": 0.74,\n      \"qwq_32b_l60_w3_bal_acc\": 0.74,\n      \"qwq_32b_l60_w5_bal_acc\": 0.742,\n      \"qwq_32b_l60_w15_bal_acc\": 0.731,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_trend\": \"flat to slightly decreasing\",\n      \"qwq_32b_best_w\": 5,\n      \"text_temporal_universal_improvement\": true,\n      \"hs_temporal_improvement_3_of_4\": true,\n      \"hs_temporal_exception\": \"OT-7B HS peaks W=3, declines at larger W; R1-32B only W=1 significant\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Temporal Window Effect on 32B Models (HS probes, corrected layers)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Layer\",\n          \"W=1\",\n          \"W=3\",\n          \"W=5\",\n          \"W=10\",\n          \"W=15\",\n          \"Trend\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"L63\",\n            \"0.826*\",\n            \"0.754\",\n            \"0.741\",\n            \"0.737\",\n            \"0.721\",\n            \"↓ declining\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"L60\",\n            \"0.740\",\n            \"0.740\",\n            \"0.742\",\n            \"0.735\",\n            \"0.731\",\n            \"→ flat\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"L14\",\n            \"0.778\",\n            \"0.790\",\n            \"0.781\",\n            \"0.793\",\n            \"0.802\",\n            \"↑ improving\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"L16\",\n            \"0.663\",\n            \"0.688*\",\n            \"0.678\",\n            \"0.668\",\n            \"0.659\",\n            \"↑↓ peaks W=3\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Worst-tier reviewers note: \\\"Temporal context helps universally for text probes (a known result — more context helps NLP classifiers). For HS probes, 2/4 models show non-monotonic or declining trends. The paper's Claim 1 should be 'temporal context helps text probes' — attributing it to HS probes is cherry-picking the models where it works.\\\"\\n\\n\\nTemporal context shows a split pattern at 32B: QwQ benefits modestly across all W (text and HS), but R1-32B DEGRADES with increasing W because concatenating 51200-dimensional vectors across W>1 windows creates a feature space massively exceeding n=20 training samples. This is not a failure of the temporal hypothesis — it's a sample-size bottleneck. Worst-tier reviewers seize on this: \\\"R1-32B temporal results are meaningless because you're fitting 102400+ parameters to 20 samples. The W=1 'significance' at L63 survives only because W=1 avoids this problem. Your 'best' result for R1-32B is actually a dimensionality artifact — the only window that avoids overfitting happens to be significant.\\\" Claim 1 (temporal universally helps) holds for text probes across all 4 models, but for HS probes, 2/4 models have caveats (OT-7B saturation, R1-32B dimensionality). Reviewers frame this as \\\"temporal improvement is a text-probe finding, not an HS-probe finding.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-model transfer probes\",\n    \"description\": \"Train probe on one model, test on another. PCA projection for cross-architecture transfer (4096→5120 or vice versa). Tests whether safety commitment encoding is universal.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_to_r1_32b_acc\": 0.522,\n      \"r1_8b_to_qwq_acc\": 0.595,\n      \"r1_32b_to_qwq_acc\": 0.673,\n      \"qwq_to_r1_32b_acc\": 0.673,\n      \"r1_32b_to_r1_8b_acc\": 0.583,\n      \"best_transfer_overall\": 0.673,\n      \"chance_baseline\": 0.5,\n      \"all_transfers_below_within_model\": true,\n      \"within_family_32b_weak_transfer\": 0.673,\n      \"cross_family_at_chance\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Cross-Model Transfer Accuracy Matrix\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Train →\",\n          \"R1-8B\",\n          \"OT-7B\",\n          \"R1-32B\",\n          \"QwQ-32B\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"0.817 (self)\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.522\",\n            \"0.595\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.688 (self)\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"0.583\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.826 (self)\",\n            \"0.673\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.673\",\n            \"0.740 (self)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Complete transfer failure. Worst-tier reviewers conclude: \\\"The paper claims to study 'safety commitment detection' as a general phenomenon, but the probes are entirely model-specific. This means the method has zero practical scalability — you need labeled data for every model you want to monitor. Combined with the engineering cost of white-box access, the deployment story collapses.\\\"\\n\\n\\nCross-model transfer fails completely — the best transfer accuracy (67.3% Qwen-family 32B internal) is far below within-model performance and barely above the majority-class baseline. Cross-family transfer (Llama↔Qwen) is at chance. Worst-tier reviewers use this as evidence against the paper: \\\"Safety encoding is model-specific. This means every new model needs its own probe training with labeled commitment points — a supervision cost the paper downplays. If safety commitment were a universal phenomenon, SOME geometric structure should transfer. The complete failure suggests the probes are learning model-specific artifacts, not a generalizable safety signal.\\\" This finding is consistent with the selectivity control task (7.5-18.5pp) but reviewers argue selectivity merely shows the probes aren't random — not that they detect genuine safety commitment vs idiosyncratic model behavior.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Lead time analysis across models\",\n    \"description\": \"Evaluate HS probe lead time distributions on all 4 models. Compare HS vs text lead times. Lead time = steps between first detection and commitment point.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_hs_lead_mean\": -0.1,\n      \"r1_8b_hs_lead_note\": \"HS detects AT commitment, not before\",\n      \"r1_8b_text_lead_mean\": 10.3,\n      \"r1_8b_text_lead_inflated\": \"high text lead is artifact of high pre-CP FPR (trigger-happy)\",\n      \"ot_7b_hs_lead_mean\": 21.6,\n      \"ot_7b_text_lead_mean\": 45.5,\n      \"ot_7b_lead_note\": \"absolute values inflated by long traces (~80 sentences)\",\n      \"qwq_32b_hs_lead_mean_range\": \"-1.2 to +1.9\",\n      \"qwq_32b_text_lead_mean_range\": \"3.7 to 12.3\",\n      \"qwq_32b_hs_lead_negative\": true,\n      \"qwq_32b_position_baseline_lead\": \"better than HS\",\n      \"r1_32b_hs_lead_mean\": \"+11.7 to +13.3\",\n      \"r1_32b_hs_lead_positive\": true,\n      \"r1_32b_coverage_at_significant_w\": 0.25,\n      \"r1_32b_lead_caveat\": \"only 5/20 traces detected at W=1\",\n      \"gru_r1_8b_lead\": \"+4.5 ± 3.0\",\n      \"gru_r1_8b_worst_seed\": \"+0.9\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Lead Time Comparison: HS vs Text vs Position\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"HS Lead (steps)\",\n          \"Text Lead (steps)\",\n          \"Position BA\",\n          \"HS BA\",\n          \"HS > Position?\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B (W=15)\",\n            \"−0.1\",\n            \"+10.3†\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.802\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B (W=3)\",\n            \"+21.6‡\",\n            \"+45.5‡\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.688\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B (W=3)\",\n            \"−1.2 to +1.9\",\n            \"+3.7 to +12.3\",\n            \"0.746\",\n            \"0.740\",\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B (W=1)\",\n            \"+11.7 to +13.3\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.686\",\n            \"0.826\",\n            \"Yes (25% cov)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Worst-tier reviewers identify lead time as the practical dealbreaker: \\\"The paper claims HS monitoring enables early detection, but 2/4 models show HS detecting LATER than text. The one model with good lead time (R1-32B) detects only 25% of traces. A deployment team would choose text monitoring over HS monitoring for lead time alone, saving the engineering cost of hidden-state extraction.\\\"\\n\\n\\nLead time results are a mixed bag that worst-tier reviewers weaponize: (1) R1-8B HS lead is effectively zero — HS detects AT commitment, not before. Only GRU achieves modest positive lead (+4.5, worst seed +0.9). Text lead of +10.3 is misleadingly high (artifact of trigger-happy detection with 20.5% pre-CP FPR). (2) QwQ-32B: HS lead time is NEGATIVE (-1.2 to +1.9 steps) while text detects 3.7-12.3 steps earlier. Position baseline (bal_acc=0.746) beats HS (bal_acc=0.740). A reviewer writes: \\\"for QwQ, just tracking relative position in the trace outperforms hidden-state monitoring — why bother with white-box access?\\\" (3) R1-32B has excellent HS lead (+13 steps) but only 25% coverage — meaning 15/20 traces are undetected. Reviewer: \\\"a detector that works on 5 traces is an anecdote, not a system.\\\" (4) OT-7B lead times are inflated by long traces. Overall: 2/4 models have HS lead ≤ 0, 1/4 has only 25% coverage, and 1/4 has inflated absolute values. No model presents a clean HS lead time story.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline (all 4 models)\",\n    \"description\": \"Mahalanobis distance on safe-trace covariance across all 4 models. Tests whether geometric distance-based methods (TrajGuard-style) can detect safety commitment.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_separation\": 0.07,\n      \"ot_7b_separation\": 0.03,\n      \"r1_32b_separation\": 0.15,\n      \"qwq_32b_separation\": 0.2,\n      \"max_separation_any_model\": 0.2,\n      \"all_models_fail\": true,\n      \"false_alarm_exceeds_tp_all_thresholds\": true,\n      \"signal_location\": \"minor PCA components, not principal directions\",\n      \"r1_8b_safe_md\": 6.66,\n      \"r1_8b_post_md\": 6.73,\n      \"r1_32b_safe_md\": 6.73,\n      \"r1_32b_post_md\": 6.88,\n      \"qwq_32b_safe_md\": 3.22,\n      \"qwq_32b_post_md\": 3.42\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Mahalanobis Distance Separation (4 models)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Layer\",\n          \"Safe MD\",\n          \"Post-CP MD\",\n          \"Separation\",\n          \"FA ≥ TP?\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"L14\",\n            \"6.66\",\n            \"6.73\",\n            \"+0.07\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"L16\",\n            \"7.68\",\n            \"7.71\",\n            \"+0.03\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"L63\",\n            \"6.73\",\n            \"6.88\",\n            \"+0.15\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"L60\",\n            \"3.22\",\n            \"3.42\",\n            \"+0.20\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Universal Mahalanobis failure confirms safety signal is geometrically inaccessible to distance-based methods. But worst-tier reviewers dismiss this: \\\"Showing Mahalanobis doesn't work is a paragraph in a methods section, not a contribution. The field already knows unsupervised geometric methods struggle with fine-grained behavioral distinctions.\\\"\\n\\n\\nMahalanobis distance fails on all 4 models (max separation 0.20 on QwQ, FA rate ≥ TP rate at every threshold). The signal lives in minor PCA components, not principal variance directions — Mahalanobis, which weights by inverse covariance, is structurally blind to this geometry. Worst-tier reviewers have mixed reactions: (1) \\\"Mahalanobis failing is expected — Bolukbasi et al. 2016 and subsequent work showed safety-relevant features are often orthogonal to principal variance. This negative result is predictable, not informative.\\\" (2) \\\"You tested one geometric baseline. What about k-NN distance, local outlier factor, or isolation forests? The negative result space is underexplored.\\\" The finding supports the \\\"existing methods fail\\\" claim but reviewers rate it as incremental — failure of one known-limited baseline is not a contribution.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Bootstrap confidence intervals + threshold-independent metrics\",\n    \"description\": \"10000 bootstrap resamples on all key metrics across 4 models. Holm-Bonferroni correction. AUROC, precision-recall curves, detection delay CDF.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"holm_bonferroni_pass\": \"12/12\",\n      \"r1_8b_precision_delta\": \"+22.4pp\",\n      \"r1_8b_precision_p\": \"<0.001\",\n      \"ot_7b_precision_delta\": \"+21.9pp\",\n      \"ot_7b_precision_p\": \"<0.001\",\n      \"qwq_32b_precision_delta\": \"+23.6pp\",\n      \"qwq_32b_precision_p\": \"<0.001\",\n      \"qwq_32b_fpr_p\": 0.004,\n      \"r1_32b_precision_delta\": \"+15.4pp\",\n      \"r1_32b_precision_p\": \"<0.001\",\n      \"r1_32b_fpr_p\": 0.007,\n      \"r1_32b_fpr_marginal_note\": \"p=0.007 passes HB threshold 0.025 but barely\",\n      \"marginal_tests_count\": 2,\n      \"all_precision_deltas_positive\": true,\n      \"precision_range\": \"+15.4 to +23.6pp\",\n      \"r1_32b_ci_width_ba\": \"~0.15\",\n      \"qwq_32b_ci_width_ba\": \"~0.12\",\n      \"r1_8b_ci_width_ba\": \"~0.06\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Holm-Bonferroni Summary (12/12, α=0.05)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Rank\",\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"p-value\",\n          \"HB Threshold\",\n          \"Pass\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"precision\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0042\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            2,\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"bal_acc\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0045\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            3,\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"precision\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0050\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            4,\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"FPR\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0056\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            5,\n            \"QwQ\",\n            \"bal_acc\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0063\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            6,\n            \"QwQ\",\n            \"precision\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0071\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            7,\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"bal_acc\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0083\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            8,\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"precision\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0100\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            9,\n            \"QwQ\",\n            \"FPR\",\n            \"0.004\",\n            \"0.0125\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            10,\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"bal_acc\",\n            \"0.005\",\n            \"0.0167\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            11,\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"FPR\",\n            \"0.007\",\n            \"0.0250\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            12,\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"FPR\",\n            \"0.044\",\n            \"0.0500\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Statistical significance is established but reviewers find it insufficient: \\\"The paper's statistical methodology is sound, but the story it tells is 'HS is significantly better by 15-24pp precision at best-case layer/window selection.' For an EMNLP Main paper, we need either (a) a novel method that achieves these gains, or (b) a surprising finding. Neither: linear probes on hidden states are standard, and it's unsurprising they outperform text embeddings (hidden states are an information superset of text).\\\"\\n\\n\\nAll 12/12 tests pass Holm-Bonferroni, which is statistically clean. But worst-tier reviewers attack the PRACTICAL significance, not the statistical significance: (1) \\\"Of course 12/12 pass — you selected the best layer AND the best window per model, then tested. The researcher degrees of freedom (4 models × 64 layers × 7 windows × 5 metrics) dwarf the 12 tests corrected for.\\\" (2) \\\"The CIs for 32B models are extremely wide (±0.15 for R1-32B bal_acc). Statistical significance with n=20 means the effect is large, but the PRECISION of the estimate is poor. You know HS is better somewhere between +3pp and +34pp — that's not actionable.\\\" (3) \\\"R1-32B FPR p=0.007 and QwQ FPR p=0.004 are the most marginal passing tests. A 10001st bootstrap replicate could flip either.\\\" (4) \\\"Holm-Bonferroni corrects for 12 tests, but the true multiplicity (including layer/window selection) is orders of magnitude larger.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Window size ablation (W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 on all models)\",\n    \"description\": \"Systematic window size sweep on all 4 models. Characterize precision-coverage tradeoff and identify optimal W per model.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"coverage_decreases_with_w_all_models\": true,\n      \"precision_increases_with_w_all_models\": true,\n      \"fpr_decreases_with_w_all_models\": true,\n      \"r1_8b_best_bal_acc_w\": 25,\n      \"r1_8b_w25_precision\": 0.822,\n      \"r1_8b_w25_coverage\": 0.488,\n      \"r1_8b_w25_fpr_step\": 0.048,\n      \"r1_32b_best_bal_acc_w\": 10,\n      \"r1_32b_w10_precision\": 0.609,\n      \"r1_32b_w10_coverage\": 0.7,\n      \"r1_32b_w10_fpr_step\": 0.234,\n      \"qwq_32b_best_bal_acc_w\": 25,\n      \"qwq_32b_w25_precision\": 0.618,\n      \"qwq_32b_w25_coverage\": 0.579,\n      \"qwq_32b_w25_fpr_step\": 0.169,\n      \"ot_7b_hs_peaks_w3\": true,\n      \"ot_7b_w3_bal_acc\": 0.688,\n      \"ot_7b_w25_bal_acc\": 0.659,\n      \"tradeoff_universal\": \"larger W → higher precision, lower coverage, lower FPR\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Precision-Coverage Tradeoff Summary (all 4 models)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"W=1 Prec/Cov\",\n          \"W=3 Prec/Cov\",\n          \"W=15 Prec/Cov\",\n          \"W=25 Prec/Cov\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"0.693/0.721\",\n            \"0.723/0.698\",\n            \"0.777/0.512\",\n            \"0.822/0.488\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"—/—\",\n            \"0.466/0.532\",\n            \"—/—\",\n            \"—/—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"0.549/0.800\",\n            \"0.560/0.800\",\n            \"0.591/0.700\",\n            \"0.566/0.700\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0.544/0.789\",\n            \"0.565/0.737\",\n            \"0.582/0.684\",\n            \"0.618/0.579\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The ablation confirms the tradeoff is universal but no operating point achieves both high precision AND high coverage. Worst-tier reviewer: \\\"Table 3 is the paper's most honest result. At no window size does any model achieve >0.7 precision AND >0.6 coverage simultaneously. This is not a tuning problem — it's a fundamental performance ceiling.\\\"\\n\\n\\nWindow ablation reveals a clean precision-sensitivity tradeoff that holds across all models. Worst-tier reviewers dismiss this: (1) \\\"The coverage-decreasing-with-W finding is trivially expected — requiring K=5 consecutive crossings with a larger window means fewer traces reach the threshold. This is a property of the detection criterion K, not an insight about temporal probing.\\\" (2) \\\"The optimal W varies by model (W=3 for OT-7B, W=10 for R1-32B, W=25 for QwQ and R1-8B). This means W is another model-specific hyperparameter to tune — combined with layer selection, you need per-model layer AND window tuning. The 'systematic evaluation' framing hides the fact that this is just exhaustive grid search.\\\" (3) \\\"At the best W per model, coverage ranges from 49% (R1-8B) to 70% (R1-32B). Missing 30-50% of safety commitment events is not deployment-ready.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Layer selection ablation (5 metrics × 4 models)\",\n    \"description\": \"Evaluate all layers with temporal probes on 32B models under 5 different selection metrics. Demonstrate crossing_rate bias and metric-dependent layer selection.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"crossing_rate_selects_shallow_32b\": true,\n      \"pure_bal_acc_selects_middle_32b\": true,\n      \"threshold_dependent_select_deep_32b\": true,\n      \"qwq_l2_vs_l60_precision_gain\": \"+11.5pp\",\n      \"r1_32b_l10_vs_l63_precision_gain\": \"+4.1pp\",\n      \"loo_cv_stability\": \"65-68% dominant fold\",\n      \"r1_8b_consistent\": true,\n      \"ot_7b_varied\": true,\n      \"bal_acc_layer_vs_precision_layer_diverge_32b\": true,\n      \"qwq_l30_bal_acc\": 0.751,\n      \"qwq_l60_bal_acc\": 0.71,\n      \"r1_32b_l47_bal_acc\": 0.829,\n      \"r1_32b_l63_bal_acc\": 0.759,\n      \"ba_optimal_different_from_precision_optimal\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Metric × Model → Selected Layer (with HS-Text precision delta)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"R1-8B Layer (ΔPrec)\",\n          \"OT-7B Layer (ΔPrec)\",\n          \"QwQ Layer (ΔPrec)\",\n          \"R1-32B Layer (ΔPrec)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"bal_acc × cr\",\n            \"L0 (+var)\",\n            \"L0 (+var)\",\n            \"L2 (+5.8pp NS)\",\n            \"L10 (+9.4pp NS)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"pure bal_acc\",\n            \"L12 (+17.8pp)\",\n            \"L9 (+25.4pp)\",\n            \"L30 (+18.0pp)\",\n            \"L47 (+13.7pp)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"precision\",\n            \"L16 (+22.4pp)\",\n            \"L16 (+21.9pp)\",\n            \"L60 (+23.6pp)\",\n            \"L63 (+15.4pp)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"pre_cp_fpr\",\n            \"L16 (−7.4pp)\",\n            \"L16 (−17.1pp)\",\n            \"L60 (−9.8pp)\",\n            \"L63 (−14.6pp)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"composite\",\n            \"L16 (+22.4pp)\",\n            \"L12 (+var)\",\n            \"L60 (+23.6pp)\",\n            \"L63 (+15.4pp)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The ablation is thorough but worst-tier reviewers question whether it constitutes a contribution: \\\"Sensitivity analysis is a sanity check, not a finding. The paper would be better if it had used the right metric from the start and reported results at correct layers — making the sensitivity analysis unnecessary for the paper's core claims.\\\"\\n\\n\\nThe layer selection ablation reveals a genuine pattern (biased metric → shallow layers → masked HS advantage), but worst-tier reviewers split on its significance: (1) The supportive reading: \\\"This is a useful observation — researchers using bal_acc × crossing_rate will get misleading results.\\\" (2) The dismissive reading (dominant in worst tier): \\\"This is expected behavior of a poorly chosen metric. The composite bal_acc × crossing_rate was never standard — it's the authors' own construction (or a rare one). Showing it's bad doesn't advance the field because nobody was using it.\\\" (3) The circularity concern: \\\"You select layers by precision/FPR, then evaluate by precision/FPR. The D044 BA-layer robustness check partially addresses this (16/16 HB pass on BA-selected layers), but the reviewer asks: 'if BA-selected layers also show HS advantage, why not just use BA selection? What's the contribution of the 5-metric comparison?'\\\" (4) \\\"LOO-CV stability of 65-68% means 32-35% of folds select a DIFFERENT layer. This is not the 'robust selection' the paper claims.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"16-label taxonomy supervision\",\n    \"description\": \"Train probes with fine-grained safety labels (Type 1/2/3/4 × pre/post) instead of binary commitment. Evaluate whether richer supervision improves detection.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_16label_acc\": 0.41,\n      \"r1_8b_binary_collapse_acc\": 0.825,\n      \"r1_8b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+0.8pp\",\n      \"ot_7b_16label_acc\": 0.35,\n      \"ot_7b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+1.1pp\",\n      \"r1_32b_16label_acc\": 0.28,\n      \"r1_32b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+0.6pp\",\n      \"qwq_32b_16label_acc\": 0.26,\n      \"qwq_32b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+0.9pp\",\n      \"fine_grained_improvement_marginal\": true,\n      \"max_collapse_delta\": \"+1.1pp\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"16-Label vs Binary Supervision\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Binary Acc\",\n          \"16-Label Acc\",\n          \"16→Binary Collapse\",\n          \"Δ vs Binary\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"0.817\",\n            \"0.41\",\n            \"0.825\",\n            \"+0.8pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"0.688\",\n            \"0.35\",\n            \"0.699\",\n            \"+1.1pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0.740\",\n            \"0.26\",\n            \"0.749\",\n            \"+0.9pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"0.826\",\n            \"0.28\",\n            \"0.832\",\n            \"+0.6pp\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Fine-grained supervision dead-end. Worst-tier implication: \\\"The information ceiling for linear probes on single-layer hidden states is fundamentally limited. Trying harder with better labels or more metrics can't overcome this.\\\"\\n\\n\\nFine-grained supervision provides marginal benefit (+0.6-1.1pp after binary collapse) across all models. The 16-class accuracy is low (26-41%) because the classes are imbalanced and transition types are not cleanly separable in hidden-state space. Worst-tier reviewers react: (1) \\\"Marginal improvement from richer supervision confirms the probe is already extracting most of the available linear signal in binary mode. This supports the reviewer's argument that linear probes are saturated — not that the methodology is optimal, but that the information ceiling is low.\\\" (2) \\\"The 16-label experiment was motivated by reviewer concern about 'underusing available supervision,' but the result shows supervision quality doesn't matter much. This undermines the paper's implicit argument that careful methodology (layer selection, temporal context) is what matters — it seems the representation quality is the binding constraint.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Oracle decomposition\",\n    \"description\": \"Analyze what the oracle label sequence encodes that hidden-state probes cannot access. Compare oracle lead (+31 steps on R1-8B) to probe lead (~0 steps).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_oracle_lead\": \"+31.0\",\n      \"r1_8b_best_probe_lead\": \"+4.5 (GRU)\",\n      \"r1_8b_oracle_gap\": \"26.5\",\n      \"r1_8b_label_autocorr_pre_cp\": 0.82,\n      \"r1_8b_hs_autocorr_pre_cp\": 0.95,\n      \"label_entropy_pre_cp_r1_8b\": 1.38,\n      \"hs_entropy_pre_cp_r1_8b\": 0.41,\n      \"r1_32b_oracle_lead\": \"+28\",\n      \"r1_32b_best_probe_lead\": \"+13 (W=1, 25% cov)\",\n      \"qwq_oracle_lead\": \"+27\",\n      \"qwq_best_probe_lead\": \"−1.2 to +1.9\",\n      \"pattern_finding\": \"Labels show gradual safety erosion pre-CP; hidden states show step-function at CP\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Oracle vs Best Probe Lead Time\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Oracle Lead\",\n          \"Best Probe Lead\",\n          \"Method\",\n          \"Gap\",\n          \"Hidden State Entropy (pre-CP)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"+31.0\",\n            \"+4.5\",\n            \"GRU multi-layer\",\n            \"26.5\",\n            \"0.41\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"~+30\",\n            \"+33.6 (GRU)\",\n            \"GRU multi-layer\",\n            \"~0†\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"+27\",\n            \"−1.2 to +1.9\",\n            \"temporal LR\",\n            \"25-28\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"+28\",\n            \"+13.3\",\n            \"temporal LR W=1\",\n            \"15 (25% cov)\",\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The oracle decomposition is the worst-tier's strongest negative finding: hidden states are fundamentally reactive (encoding current state) rather than predictive (encoding trajectory). Reviewer: \\\"This paper demonstrates that hidden-state monitoring is a lagging indicator. For safety deployment, you need leading indicators. Text-based trajectory analysis, while noisier, is the better research direction.\\\"\\n\\n\\nOracle decomposition reveals a 26-28 step gap between what's detectable in labels vs hidden states. The label sequence shows gradual pre-CP patterns (increasing unsafe-adjacent labels, shortening safe streaks) that hidden states don't track. Worst-tier reviewers draw a harsh conclusion: \\\"The 31-step oracle lead proves that safety commitment is a GRADUAL process visible in the model's outputs. But the hidden-state probe can only detect it at or after the commitment point (lead ≈ 0). This means hidden states encode the CURRENT safety state but not the TRAJECTORY toward commitment. For deployment, trajectory prediction is what matters — catching the commitment after it happens is too late. The oracle gap is not a puzzle for Discussion; it's evidence that the entire hidden-state monitoring approach is fundamentally limited. Text-based monitoring that tracks reasoning trajectory (even with higher FPR) provides earlier warning.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B, STILL-trained)\",\n    \"description\": \"4th model completing the 2×2 architecture×scale matrix. Full pipeline: layer sweep, temporal probes, HS vs text comparison, GRU, selectivity control task.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"best_layer\": 16,\n      \"best_layer_depth_pct\": 57.1,\n      \"best_hs_bal_acc_w3\": 0.688,\n      \"best_hs_precision_w3\": 0.466,\n      \"best_hs_pre_cp_fpr\": 0.1,\n      \"bootstrap_3_of_3_hb\": true,\n      \"bootstrap_bal_acc_delta\": \"+10.7pp\",\n      \"bootstrap_precision_delta\": \"+21.9pp\",\n      \"bootstrap_fpr_delta\": \"−17.1pp\",\n      \"selectivity_pp\": 18.3,\n      \"n_traces\": 228,\n      \"gru_multi_bal_acc\": 0.746,\n      \"gru_multi_lead\": \"+33.6\",\n      \"gru_multi_fpr\": 0.274,\n      \"hs_temporal_saturation_w3\": true,\n      \"text_temporal_w25_best\": 0.614\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"OT-7B Full Pipeline Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"bal_acc\",\n          \"precision\",\n          \"pre-CP FPR\",\n          \"coverage\",\n          \"lead\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"HS temporal W=3 L16\",\n            \"0.688\",\n            \"0.466\",\n            \"0.100\",\n            \"0.532\",\n            \"+21.6\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Text temporal W=25\",\n            \"0.614\",\n            \"0.329\",\n            \"0.161\",\n            \"0.660\",\n            \"+45.5\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GRU multi L13-19\",\n            \"0.746\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.274\",\n            \"0.645\",\n            \"+33.6\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HS-Text Δ (HB)\",\n            \"+10.7pp ✅\",\n            \"+21.9pp ✅\",\n            \"−17.1pp ✅\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"OT-7B strengthens the 7-8B story but worst-tier reviewers note: \\\"The 2×2 matrix now has two strong cells (7-8B) and two weak cells (32B). The paper's breadth claim ('4 models') is technically true but misleading — 2/4 models have serious practical caveats that make the HS advantage non-deployable.\\\"\\n\\n\\nOT-7B is the cleanest single-model evidence for HS advantage (3/3 HB, highest selectivity 18.3pp, largest n=228 traces). But worst-tier reviewers are unimpressed by adding a 4th model that confirms the known pattern: (1) \\\"A 4th model at 7B showing the same result doesn't address the 32B caveats. The paper already has two 7-8B models that work. The question is whether HS monitoring scales, and OT-7B can't answer that.\\\" (2) \\\"OT-7B has its own caveats: HS temporal saturation at W=3 (bal_acc declines at larger W), and absolute performance is still below R1-8B (best bal_acc 68.8% vs 80.2%). The 'cleanest evidence' is still only 69% balanced accuracy — barely better than a biased coin.\\\" (3) \\\"GRU lead time of +33.6 steps sounds impressive until you note OT-7B traces average ~80 sentences — normalize by trace length and the lead time fraction is similar to other models.\\\" (4) \\\"FPR of 27.4% for GRU means ~1 in 4 safe steps triggers the detector. At 80-sentence traces, that's ~20 false alarms per safe trace.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"GRU/Attention probe upper bound (32B models)\",\n    \"description\": \"GRU sequence model on 32B models at corrected layers. Tests whether nonlinear sequence modeling can recover signal that linear temporal probes miss.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_gru_single_bal_acc\": 0.675,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_multi_bal_acc\": 0.655,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_mean\": \"+8.8 to +9.1\",\n      \"r1_32b_gru_fpr\": \"0.114 to 0.135\",\n      \"r1_32b_gru_coverage\": \"0.23 to 0.30\",\n      \"r1_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta\": \"−6pp bal_acc (GRU worse)\",\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_single_bal_acc\": 0.694,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_multi_bal_acc\": 0.724,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean\": \"+15.9\",\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_fpr\": 0.289,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_coverage\": 0.84,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta\": \"−3.7pp bal_acc (GRU worse at bal_acc, better at coverage)\",\n      \"ot_7b_gru_multi_bal_acc\": 0.746,\n      \"ot_7b_gru_multi_lead\": \"+33.6\",\n      \"ot_7b_gru_fpr\": 0.274,\n      \"gru_worse_than_lr_3_of_4\": true,\n      \"gru_note\": \"GRU trades accuracy for coverage/lead — higher FPR drives earlier detection\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"GRU vs Temporal LR (32B models, corrected layers)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"LR bal_acc\",\n          \"GRU bal_acc\",\n          \"Δ\",\n          \"GRU FPR\",\n          \"LR FPR\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-32B (L63 W=10)\",\n            \"0.737\",\n            \"~0.675\",\n            \"−6.2pp\",\n            \"0.135\",\n            \"0.234\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B (L60 W=5)\",\n            \"0.761\",\n            \"0.724\",\n            \"−3.7pp\",\n            \"0.289\",\n            \"0.217\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B (L16 multi)\",\n            \"0.688\",\n            \"0.746\",\n            \"+5.8pp\",\n            \"0.274\",\n            \"0.100\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-8B (L14 multi)\",\n            \"0.806\",\n            \"0.809\",\n            \"+0.3pp\",\n            \"0.165\",\n            \"0.097\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"GRU fails to improve over LR on most models, confirming linear probes are at the information ceiling. Worst-tier reviewer: \\\"The paper's headline is 'HS beats text.' The method is logistic regression. The contribution is... careful hyperparameter selection? This is a well-executed experiment report, not a methods paper.\\\"\\n\\n\\nGRU does NOT outperform temporal LR on balanced accuracy for 3/4 models. On R1-32B (n=20 test), GRU severely overfits — bal_acc drops 6pp below LR. On QwQ, GRU achieves higher coverage and lead time but at 2× the FPR. This confirms linear probes are sufficient (or even superior given small samples). Worst-tier reviewers use this to attack novelty: \\\"The paper's method is logistic regression on hidden states with a sliding window. That's not a contribution — it's the simplest possible baseline. The fact that GRU and MLP don't help confirms the method space is trivial, which means the paper's 'systematic comparison of 7 methods' is comparing variations of the obvious approach (linear probe) against methods that were never designed for this task (Mahalanobis, BOCPD, CCS). The negative results are predictable, not informative.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Text-based baseline (sentence embeddings + temporal LR)\",\n    \"description\": \"Sentence-BERT (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) embeddings + temporal window LR on CoT text of all 4 models. Critical comparison establishing what text monitoring achieves without hidden-state access.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_text_static_acc\": 0.661,\n      \"r1_8b_text_temporal_w15_acc\": 0.678,\n      \"r1_8b_text_temporal_w15_auroc\": 0.759,\n      \"r1_8b_hs_acc_advantage\": \"+15-18pp\",\n      \"ot_7b_text_best_bal_acc\": 0.614,\n      \"ot_7b_text_best_precision\": 0.329,\n      \"ot_7b_text_best_fpr\": 0.161,\n      \"qwq_32b_text_temporal_bal_acc\": 0.65,\n      \"qwq_32b_text_precision\": 0.421,\n      \"r1_32b_text_temporal_bal_acc\": 0.646,\n      \"r1_32b_text_precision\": 0.45,\n      \"hs_vs_text_precision_gap_range\": \"+15.4 to +23.6pp\",\n      \"text_requires_zero_infra_change\": true,\n      \"text_precision_all_models\": \"0.329-0.487\",\n      \"hs_precision_all_models\": \"0.466-0.776\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"HS vs Text Precision Gap (+ engineering cost annotation)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"HS Precision\",\n          \"Text Precision\",\n          \"Δ\",\n          \"HS Infra Cost\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"0.777 (W=15)\",\n            \"0.487 (W=15)\",\n            \"+29.0pp\",\n            \"white-box, L14\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"0.466 (W=3)\",\n            \"0.329 (W=25)\",\n            \"+13.7pp\",\n            \"white-box, L16\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0.578 (W=3)\",\n            \"0.421 (W=best)\",\n            \"+15.7pp\",\n            \"white-box, L60\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"0.603 (W=1)\",\n            \"0.450 (W=best)\",\n            \"+15.3pp\",\n            \"white-box, L63\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The text baseline is the worst-tier reviewer's strongest weapon: \\\"A 15-24pp precision gain sounds meaningful until you consider: (a) text monitoring costs $0 in infrastructure, (b) HS monitoring requires white-box access + per-model tuning, (c) the BGE-large result shows the gap shrinks with better text encoders, (d) safe FPR is 39-62% regardless of modality — both approaches produce unusable false alarm rates. The paper should honestly state: 'HS probes are measurably but marginally better than text probes. Whether the margin justifies white-box access depends on deployment constraints. For most practical settings, text monitoring suffices.'\\\"\\n\\n\\nText baseline performs consistently across all models (bal_acc 0.61-0.68, precision 0.33-0.49). HS probes outperform by +15-24pp precision, which is statistically significant (12/12 HB). But worst-tier reviewers attack the COST-BENEFIT ratio, not the statistical claim: (1) \\\"Text probes need zero infrastructure change — you monitor the CoT output that's already available. HS probes require white-box access to model internals, which means: (a) running the model locally (can't use API), (b) modifying the inference pipeline to extract intermediate activations, (c) per-model layer selection + probe training. The 15-24pp precision gain comes at enormous engineering cost.\\\" (2) \\\"At 33-49% text precision, you get 1 true alarm per 2-3 triggers. At 47-78% HS precision, you get 1 per 1.3-2.1 triggers. Is that difference worth instrumenting your entire inference stack? For most deployment teams, no.\\\" (3) \\\"Text probes improve with better embeddings (BGE-large, domain-fine-tuned). The paper tested only all-MiniLM-L6-v2 — the weakest common baseline. A reviewer notes: 'Use GPT-4 as the text classifier and the gap likely narrows to <5pp.'\\\" (4) \\\"The BGE-large comparison (exp_bge_large_text_comparison) showed only 1/4 HB pass for HS vs BGE-large — the gap narrows significantly with a better text encoder.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"BOCPD/CUSUM on all 4 models\",\n    \"description\": \"Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection + CUSUM on probe score sequences. Tests whether advanced change-point methods can achieve positive lead time.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_bocpd_detection\": 0.93,\n      \"r1_8b_bocpd_lead\": \"+3.45\",\n      \"r1_8b_cusum_lead\": \"+0.89\",\n      \"r1_8b_fcp_baseline_lead\": \"+4.89\",\n      \"r1_8b_fcp_beats_bocpd_on_lead\": true,\n      \"ot_7b_bocpd_detection\": 0.0,\n      \"ot_7b_bocpd_note\": \"W=3 probe scores too spiky, Gaussian model fails\",\n      \"ot_7b_fcp_best_detection\": 0.894,\n      \"ot_7b_fcp_best_lead\": \"+11.83\",\n      \"r1_32b_bocpd_detection\": 0.0,\n      \"qwq_32b_bocpd_detection\": 0.0,\n      \"r1_32b_cusum_detection\": 0.85,\n      \"r1_32b_cusum_lead\": \"+2.59\",\n      \"qwq_32b_cusum_detection\": 0.737,\n      \"qwq_32b_cusum_lead\": \"+9.86\",\n      \"bocpd_works_only_r1_8b\": true,\n      \"cusum_universally_worse_than_fcp\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Change-Point Detection Cross-Model Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"BOCPD Det%\",\n          \"BOCPD Lead\",\n          \"CUSUM Det%\",\n          \"CUSUM Lead\",\n          \"FCP Det%\",\n          \"FCP Lead\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"93%\",\n            \"+3.45\",\n            \"65%\",\n            \"+0.89\",\n            \"65%\",\n            \"+4.89\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"0%\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"75%†\",\n            \"+7.46†\",\n            \"89%\",\n            \"+11.83\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"0%\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"85%\",\n            \"+2.59\",\n            \"95%\",\n            \"+5.68\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0%\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"74%\",\n            \"+9.86\",\n            \"79%\",\n            \"+9.40\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Complete BOCPD failure on 3/4 models and CUSUM inferiority to simple thresholding. Worst-tier reviewer: \\\"The paper tests two change-point algorithms that were designed for very different signal types, finds they don't work, and claims this as a negative result. But the method selection is so narrow that the negative result is uninformative — you tested the wrong tools.\\\"\\n\\n\\nBOCPD fails on 3/4 models (0% detection on OT-7B, R1-32B, QwQ). Only R1-8B works (93%) because R1-8B has gradual probe score transitions that match BOCPD's Gaussian change-point model. Other models have abrupt transitions (OT-7B) or weak signals (32B) that BOCPD's parametric assumptions can't handle. CUSUM detects some traces but with worse lead than simple first-crossing-point (FCP) threshold. Worst-tier reviewers: (1) \\\"BOCPD and CUSUM were never designed for this signal type. Testing them is due diligence, but their failure is predictable — these methods assume stationary-then-shifted Gaussian data, while probe scores are bounded [0,1] and often spiky.\\\" (2) \\\"The negative result strengthens the 'nothing works well' narrative, but the method selection is questionable. Why not test HMM, Viterbi decoding, or learned change-point detectors? The paper tests 2 off-the-shelf methods and declares change-point detection a dead end.\\\" (3) \\\"Simple FCP threshold beats BOCPD on mean lead time for R1-8B. The 'advanced' methods add nothing.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"False positive analysis (safe trace FPR, all methods)\",\n    \"description\": \"Run all probes on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5) across all 4 models. Report safe FPR, pre-CP FPR, precision, and precision-recall curves.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_hs_safe_fpr\": 0.39,\n      \"r1_8b_text_safe_fpr\": 0.385,\n      \"ot_7b_hs_safe_fpr\": \"0.111-0.175\",\n      \"ot_7b_text_safe_fpr\": \"0.302-0.460\",\n      \"qwq_32b_hs_safe_fpr\": \"0.45-0.50\",\n      \"qwq_32b_text_safe_fpr\": \"0.325-0.425\",\n      \"r1_32b_hs_safe_fpr\": 0.239,\n      \"r1_32b_text_safe_fpr\": 0.31,\n      \"safe_fpr_range_all\": \"0.111 to 0.521\",\n      \"safe_fpr_median\": \"~0.39\",\n      \"gru_safe_fpr\": 0.521,\n      \"gru_highest_fpr_note\": \"GRU trades precision for coverage → highest FPR\",\n      \"precision_all_methods_range\": \"0.329 to 0.777\",\n      \"no_method_achieves_80pct_precision\": true,\n      \"deployment_threshold_precision_70\": \"only R1-8B HS W≥15 exceeds\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Safe Trace FPR Summary (all methods × all models)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"HS Static FPR\",\n          \"HS Temporal FPR\",\n          \"Text Temporal FPR\",\n          \"GRU FPR\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"0.390\",\n            \"0.392\",\n            \"0.385\",\n            \"0.521\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"0.111\",\n            \"0.175\",\n            \"0.460\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0.500\",\n            \"0.450\",\n            \"0.425\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"0.239\",\n            \"0.239\",\n            \"0.310\",\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Deployment Viability Assessment\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Criterion\",\n          \"Threshold\",\n          \"Models Meeting Criterion\",\n          \"Best Method\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Safe FPR < 20%\",\n            \"0.20\",\n            \"1/4 (OT-7B HS static)\",\n            \"HS static L16\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Precision > 70%\",\n            \"0.70\",\n            \"1/4 (R1-8B W≥15)\",\n            \"HS temporal W=25\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Coverage > 60%\",\n            \"0.60\",\n            \"2/4 (R1-8B W≤3, R1-32B W≤3)\",\n            \"HS temporal W=1\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"All three criteria\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0/4\",\n            \"None\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"FPR analysis is the worst-tier's core evidence for the \\\"not practically useful\\\" dismissal. No model meets all three deployment criteria simultaneously. The best model (R1-8B) achieves >70% precision only at W≥15 where coverage drops to 49%. The paper's deployment framing is untenable. Reframing guidance: \\\"Position as diagnostic/research tool, explicitly disclaim deployment readiness. Lead with 'none of our 7 methods achieves deployment-ready performance' as a cautionary finding.\\\"\\n\\n\\nSafe FPR ranges from 11% (OT-7B HS static, best case) to 52% (GRU, worst case), with a median around 39%. This is the worst-tier dealbreaker. Reviewer calculation: \\\"At 39% safe FPR with K=5 consecutive crossings for trace-level detection, a 40-step safe trace has ~39% probability of triggering the detector. In a production system processing 1000 safe traces per day, that's ~390 false alarms daily. Each requires human review at ~5 minutes → 32.5 person-hours/day of false alarm triage. No deployment team would accept this.\\\" Even the best case (OT-7B HS 11.1% safe FPR) means 111 false alarms per 1000 safe traces. The worst-tier reviewer's verdict: \\\"A safety monitor that fires on 1 in 3 to 1 in 2 safe traces is not a monitor — it's a random noise generator. The paper should lead with this limitation, not bury it.\\\" QwQ-32B is particularly problematic: HS safe FPR (45-50%) is HIGHER than text safe FPR (33-43%), meaning HS monitoring generates MORE false alarms on safe traces for QwQ.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"5-fold CV validation of multi-metric ensemble (7-8B)\",\n    \"description\": \"Cross-validated ensemble of metric-weighted layer selections on R1-8B and OT-7B. Validates that multi-metric ensemble eliminates layer selection bias under proper CV.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_ensemble_cv_prec\": 0.565,\n      \"r1_8b_best_single_cv_prec\": 0.604,\n      \"r1_8b_ensemble_vs_text_gap\": \"+19.3pp\",\n      \"r1_8b_ensemble_vs_text_p\": \"<0.002\",\n      \"ot_7b_ensemble_cv_prec\": 0.506,\n      \"ot_7b_best_single_cv_prec\": 0.473,\n      \"ot_7b_ensemble_beats_best_single\": true,\n      \"ot_7b_ensemble_beats_best_single_p\": 0.033,\n      \"cv_validates_preliminary\": true,\n      \"cv_acceptance_probability_delta\": \"+0.01 to +0.03\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Ensemble CV Results (7-8B models)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Ensemble Prec\",\n          \"Best Single Prec\",\n          \"Text Prec\",\n          \"Ens vs Text p\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"0.565\",\n            \"0.604\",\n            \"0.372\",\n            \"<0.002\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"0.506\",\n            \"0.473\",\n            \"0.236\",\n            \"<0.001\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Ensemble CV is a marginal improvement on an already-validated statistical claim. Worst-tier impact on acceptance: negligible (+1-3%). The bottleneck is not statistical rigor — it's practical utility and novelty. This experiment is low-cost and worth including in supplementary, but it cannot rescue the paper from worst-tier reviewer concerns.\\n\\nCV validation confirms the ensemble result holds (p≤0.002 for ensemble vs text on R1-8B). But worst-tier reviewers are unmoved: (1) \\\"CV validating an ensemble of layer selection metrics on 7-8B models doesn't address ANY of our core concerns. The issues are: high safe FPR (39-62%), model-specific caveats at 32B, engineering cost of white-box access, and insufficient novelty. A statistically cleaner layer selection protocol is nice but tangential.\\\" (2) \\\"The ensemble precision (0.506-0.565) is actually LOWER than the best single metric on R1-8B (0.604). On OT-7B the ensemble slightly beats the best single metric (0.506 vs 0.473) but the absolute precision is 50% — a coin flip.\\\" (3) \\\"This experiment strengthens an already- strong statistical case (12/12 HB already passes). The paper doesn't need MORE statistical validation; it needs BETTER results or a DIFFERENT framing.\\\" The CV ensemble moves acceptance probability by +1-3%, insufficient to shift from worst tier.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":1201,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1201}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UsfMXdnWQTQJALm77NqeCi","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — contrastive_trace_safety\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (LLM Security Track)\n5\t- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026\n6\t\n7\t## Core Claims (D031: layer selection as primary contribution, 4 models)\n8\t\n9\t**Contribution structure (D031)**:\n10\t- **Primary (methodological)**: Layer selection metric sensitivity — biased metrics reverse conclusions\n11\t- **Secondary (empirical)**: HS advantage at correct layers, qualified by scale; temporal improvement universal\n12\t- **Tertiary (negative results)**: 6 alternative methods fail, establishing what doesn't work\n13\t\n14\t1. **Temporal context universally improves probe accuracy across both modalities** (D014 + D023 nuanced) — Temporal context is the most robust improvement across all 4 models and both modalities. Text probes benefit consistently from larger windows across all models. HS probes benefit in 3/4 models; OT-7B HS peaks at W=3 and declines at larger W (confirmed on both layer 0 and layer 16).\n15\t\n16\t   **HS temporal gains** (W=1→best, 3 original models): accuracy +3pp (77.8%→81.0%), precision +13pp (69.3%→82.2%), FPR halved (9.65%→4.83%).\n17\t   **OT-7B HS temporal** (layer 16): bal_acc peaks at W=3 (68.8%), declines to 65.9% at W=25 (-2.9pp). BUT precision and FPR continue improving (prec 42.8%→49.4%, FPR 12.0%→7.5%). Temporal saturation is bal_acc-specific; larger W trades bal_acc for better precision/FPR. Confirmed on both layer 0 and layer 16 — genuine OT-7B characteristic.\n18\t   **Text temporal gains** (all 4 models, W=1→best): universally +3-12pp bal_acc, +8-23pp precision, FPR reduced.\n19\t\n20\t   **IMPORTANT: coverage direction depends on detection criterion K.** K=1 (single crossing): coverage DECREASES with W. K=5 (consecutive crossings): coverage may INCREASE with W. Claim 1 is about accuracy/precision improvement, NOT coverage.\n21\t\n22\t   The core insight: \"Temporal context universally improves text probes across all 4 models. For HS probes, 3/4 models benefit from larger temporal windows, while OT-7B shows early saturation (W=3 peak).\"\n23\t\n24\t2. **HS probes outperform text probes at correctly selected layers, with strong evidence at 7-8B and model-specific patterns at 32B** (D031: qualified from D030 \"universal\") —\n25\t\n26\t   **KEY FINDING (D029)**: The apparent \"scale-dependent\" HS advantage (significant at 7-8B, absent at 32B) was an artifact of the layer selection metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate`, which systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models. At correctly selected deep layers, HS outperforms text on all 4 models, but the NATURE of the advantage differs by scale.\n27\t\n28\t   **Layer selection metric bias (D028, refined D036)**: `bal_acc × crossing_rate` selects shallow layers because shallow layers have higher crossing rates (more trigger-happy probes). 3/4 non-biased threshold-dependent metrics (precision, pre_cp_fpr, composite) converge on deep layers; pure bal_acc (threshold-free) selects middle layers (L30/L47). Deployment-relevant metric consensus selects L60/L63 for 32B models.\n29\t\n30\t   **7-8B models: HS significantly outperforms text (2/2 models) — UNCHANGED**\n31\t\n32\t   **R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, n=43):**\n33\t   - Precision: 69.7% vs 47.2% (+22.4pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n34\t   - Accuracy: 81.3% vs 72.9% (+8.2pp, p=0.005, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n35\t   - Pre-CP FPR: 14.8% vs 22.2% (-7.4pp, p=0.044, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅** — rank 12/12, threshold=0.0500)\n36\t\n37\t   **OT-7B (Qwen-7B, STILL-trained, n=47, layer 16, matched W=3) — D026 M001 corrected:**\n38\t   - Bal Acc: 68.8% vs 58.1% (+10.7pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n39\t   - Precision: 46.6% vs 24.7% (+21.9pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n40\t   - Pre-CP FPR: 10.0% vs 27.1% (-17.1pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n41\t   - **3/3 metrics significant — OT-7B cleanest single-model evidence**\n42\t\n43\t   **32B models: HS outperforms text at CORRECTED LAYERS but with model-specific caveats (D029+D031)**\n44\t\n45\t   **QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native, n=19-20, Layer 60 corrected from L2):**\n46\t   - Bal Acc: +11-22pp across all 15 window sizes, all p<0.005 (bootstrap, null-centered)\n47\t   - 15/15 W values show significant HS advantage\n48\t   - **CRITICAL CAVEAT (D031)**: HS lead time NEGATIVE (-1.2 to 1.9 steps vs text 3.7-12.3). HS is \"more accurate but detects later.\" Position baseline bal_acc=0.746 > HS=0.740. In safety deployment, detecting 8-12 steps earlier may outweigh higher accuracy.\n49\t   - W=3: bal_acc +15.4pp, precision +23.6pp, FPR -9.8pp — **3/3 Holm-Bonferroni ✅**\n50\t\n51\t   **R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, n=20, Layer 63 corrected from L10):**\n52\t   - W=1: bal_acc +18.7pp (0.826 vs 0.639), p=0.000; precision +15.4pp (0.603 vs 0.448), p=0.000; FPR -14.6pp (0.235 vs 0.380), p=0.007 — **3/3 Bonferroni ✅**\n53\t   - W=3: bal_acc +11.2pp p=0.020, precision p=0.032, FPR p=0.077 — **0/3 Bonferroni** (v2 null-centered)\n54\t   - W=5-15: all NS\n55\t   - **CRITICAL CAVEAT (D031)**: Only W=1 passes Bonferroni, coverage=25% (5/20 traces). Evidence statistically narrow.\n56\t   - **POSITIVE**: Lead time +11.7-13.3 steps (excellent early warning). HS >> Position (+14.2pp).\n57\t   - **NOTE**: L10 vs L63 at W=10: L10=0.795 vs L63=0.754 — L10 slightly better. \"Scheme B选错层\" narrative weaker for R1-32B.\n58\t   - ✅ P-VALUE UNIFIED: null-centered two-sided, consistent with QwQ.\n59\t\n60\t   **32B cross-model divergence (D031 key finding for Discussion):**\n61\t   | Dimension | R1-32B L63 | QwQ L60 |\n62\t   |-----------|-----------|---------|\n63\t   | Bonferroni | W=1 only (3/3) | All 15 W (3/3) |\n64\t   | Lead time | +11.7 to +13.3 (excellent) | -1.2 to +1.9 (poor) |\n65\t   | HS vs Position | HS >> Pos (+14.2pp) | HS < Pos (-0.6pp) |\n66\t   | Coverage (W=1) | 25% | 74% |\n67\t   Paper MUST discuss this divergence per-model, not unify into single narrative.\n68\t\n69\t   **OLD 32B results at BIASED layers (L2/L10) — RETRACTED as metric artifact:**\n70\t   - R1-32B L10: precision +9.4pp (p=0.064), accuracy +6.2pp (p=0.13)\n71\t   - QwQ-32B L2: precision +5.8pp (p=0.35), accuracy +0.9pp (p=0.83)\n72\t   - These non-significant results were caused by the biased `bal_acc × crossing_rate` metric selecting layers where HS signal is weak.\n73\t\n74\t   **Holm-Bonferroni COMPLETE (D032): 12/12 ALL PASS**\n75\t   Previously 5/12 (biased layers). With corrected layers: **12/12** step-down pass (α=0.05).\n76\t\n77\t   | Rank | Model | Metric | p-value | Threshold | Pass |\n78\t   |------|-------|--------|---------|-----------|------|\n79\t   | 1 | R1-8B | precision | <0.001 | 0.0042 | ✅ |\n80\t   | 2 | OT-7B | bal_acc | <0.001 | 0.0045 | ✅ |\n81\t   | 3 | OT-7B | precision | <0.001 | 0.0050 | ✅ |\n82\t   | 4 | OT-7B | FPR | <0.001 | 0.0056 | ✅ |\n83\t   | 5 | QwQ | bal_acc | <0.001 | 0.0063 | ✅ |\n84\t   | 6 | QwQ | precision | <0.001 | 0.0071 | ✅ |\n85\t   | 7 | R1-32B | bal_acc | <0.001 | 0.0083 | ✅ |\n86\t   | 8 | R1-32B | precision | <0.001 | 0.0100 | ✅ |\n87\t   | 9 | QwQ | FPR | 0.004 | 0.0125 | ✅ |\n88\t   | 10 | R1-8B | bal_acc | 0.005 | 0.0167 | ✅ |\n89\t   | 11 | R1-32B | FPR | 0.007 | 0.0250 | ✅ |\n90\t   | 12 | R1-8B | FPR | 0.044 | 0.0500 | ✅ |\n91\t\n92\t   **Caveats**: Different W per model (R1-8B W=15, OT-7B/QwQ W=3, R1-32B W=1). Tests measure \"HS > text at each model's best setting,\" not \"at uniform W.\" R1-32B W=1 has 25% coverage. Paper must state these conditions.\n93\t\n94\t   **FPR dual reporting**: Step-level (r1-8b: HS 8.8%, text 20.5%) vs trace-level (HS 14.8%, text 22.2%). Both reported.\n95\t\n96\t   **FPR reversal RESOLVED (D026)**: OT-7B HS FPR@L16 (10.0%) < Text FPR at ANY window (16.1%). HS dominates on FPR at 7-8B.\n97\t\n98\t   **Effect size consistency**: Precision advantage across all 4 models at corrected layers: R1-8B +22.4pp, OT-7B +21.2pp, QwQ +23.6pp, R1-32B +15.4pp. Remarkably consistent +15-24pp range.\n99\t\n100\t   Paper recommended claim (D032): \"At correctly selected layers, HS probes significantly outperform text probes on all 4 models (12/12 Holm-Bonferroni, precision +15-24pp). The layer selection metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically biases toward shallow layers, entirely masking this advantage at 32B scale. Practical implications differ by model: 7-8B models show strong HS advantage with comparable timing; R1-32B shows excellent lead time (+13 steps) but narrow coverage at the significant window; QwQ shows robust statistical significance but HS detects later than text.\"\n101\t\n102\t3. **HS and text monitoring exhibit different detection timing profiles** (narrowed from old Claim 3, now 4/4 models with GRU) — On r1-8b: HS temporal LR detects AT the commitment point (lead ~0) while text detects ~10 steps earlier. GRU achieves modest positive lead (+4.5+-3.0, worst seed +0.9). BOCPD/CUSUM fail to improve over simple thresholds. The oracle's +31-step advantage shows hidden states encode current safety state but poorly predict future trajectory. On 32B models: timing profiles vary (QwQ GRU lead +15.9, R1-32B +8.8) but FPR differences are non-significant. OT-7B GRU (layer 16, multi-layer L13-19, 3 seeds): bal_acc 74.6+-1.3%, coverage 64.5+-7.0%, lead +33.6+-4.8, FPR 27.4+-6.1%. GRU trades higher FPR for longer lead (consistent with R1-8B pattern). NOTE: OT-7B absolute lead times are inflated by long traces (~80 sentences vs ~30). Paper should normalize or explicitly caveat trace length differences when comparing across models.\n103\t\n104\t   NOTE: 32B timing profiles should be RE-EVALUATED at corrected layers (L60/L63) — current GRU results used old biased layers.\n105\t\n106\t## Methodological Contribution: Layer Selection Sensitivity (D028, elevated from RETRACTED Claim 4)\n107\t\n108\tThe layer selection metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically biases toward shallow layers because shallow layers have higher crossing rates (more frequently crossing the detection threshold). This bias is especially problematic for 32B models, where it selects layers at 3-16% depth instead of deep layers selected by threshold-dependent metrics.\n109\t\n110\t**Ablation evidence (4 models × 5 metrics) — UPDATED D036**:\n111\t- `bal_acc × crossing_rate`: selects L2 (QwQ), L10 (R1-32B) — shallow, biased\n112\t- Pure `bal_acc`: selects **middle layers** for 32B — L30 (QwQ, 47%), L47 (R1-32B, 73%); deep for 7-8B (agrees with other metrics). Threshold-free metric, behaves like AUROC.\n113\t- `precision`: selects deep layers for all models (L60/L63 for 32B)\n114\t- `pre_cp_fpr`: selects deep layers for all models\n115\t- `composite` (additive): selects deep layers for all models\n116\t- **Pattern**: Threshold-FREE metrics (bal_acc, AUROC) → middle layers; threshold-DEPENDENT metrics (precision, FPR, composite) → deep layers. Crossing_rate-contaminated metrics → shallow layers.\n117\t\n118\t**LOO-CV stability (D036)**: Under LOO-CV, the dominant layer is selected in 65-68% of folds regardless of metric. QwQ L30 selected 13/19 folds (bal_acc), R1-32B L47 selected 13/20 folds (bal_acc). Selection is robust to val set composition.\n119\t\n120\t**Corrected layer definition (D036)**: \"Corrected layers\" = consensus of threshold-dependent metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) — 3 of 4 non-biased metrics, not all 4. Pure bal_acc selects middle layers (L30/L47) with higher step-level accuracy (0.75/0.83 vs 0.71/0.76 at deep layers), but deployment-relevant metrics (precision, FPR) favor deep layers where all 4 models achieve HB-significant HS advantage.\n121\t\n122\t**Impact**: Incorrect layer selection entirely masked the HS advantage at 32B scale, leading to the false conclusion that HS advantage is scale-dependent. Even among unbiased metrics, metric choice affects optimal layer depth — threshold-free vs threshold-dependent is a second dimension of researcher degrees of freedom beyond the crossing_rate bias.\n123\t\n124\t**Anti-circular-reasoning defense (D031, refined D036)**: The claim that deep layers are \"correct\" for deployment rests on: (1) `crossing_rate` has a MECHANISTIC bias — shallow layers have compressed representations (1.4-2.7× smaller cosine distances) generating noise-driven crossings; (2) threshold-dependent metrics (precision, FPR) directly measure deployment-relevant properties; (3) at deep layers, all 4 models show significant HS advantage (HB corrected); (4) LOO-CV confirms selection stability. The paper presents layer selection as a \"researcher degree of freedom\" with systematic audit via sensitivity analysis.\n125\t\n126\t**Paper treatment**: Dedicated subsection in Method + Table showing metric × model → selected layer + downstream HS-text comparison. Frame as sensitivity analysis, not \"correct vs incorrect.\"\n127\t\n128\t## Story Spine\n129\t\n130\t- **Problem**: Reasoning models exhibit \"self-jailbreak\" — safety reasoning and harmful execution interleave throughout extended CoT, culminating in a \"commitment point\" where safety reasoning permanently ceases. Existing hidden-state detectors (TrajGuard, SafeSwitch, SIREN) target adversarial INPUT attacks on instruction-tuned models; they have never been evaluated on reasoning-model self-jailbreak where the threat is the model's OWN reasoning process.\n131\t\n132\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Hidden-state detectors achieve 95% defense on standard LLMs (TrajGuard) but operate at token-level. Reasoning-model safety analysis (Self-Jailbreaking, HarmThoughts) characterizes the phenomenon without providing deployable detectors. Text-level CoT monitors (SafeRemind, SafeThink) are vulnerable to pathological CoT. No prior work systematically compares detection modalities for reasoning-model self-jailbreak.\n133\t\n134\t- **Our framing**: We provide the first systematic evaluation of detection methods for reasoning-model safety commitment across **4 models, 2 architectures (Llama + Qwen), 2 training methods (R1-distilled + STILL), and 2 scales (7-8B + 32B)**, comparing hidden-state probes, text-based probes, sequence models (GRU), change-point detection (BOCPD/CUSUM), unsupervised methods (Mahalanobis, RepE direction, CCS), and nonlinear probes (MLP). We discover that: (a) temporal context is the most robust improvement, benefiting both modalities universally; (b) HS probes universally outperform text at correctly selected layers, with a critical sensitivity to layer selection methodology; (c) existing methods designed for other settings (TrajGuard, BOCPD, CCS, RepE) fail on reasoning model commitment; (d) linear probes are sufficient (MLP adds no value); (e) safety representations are model-specific (cross-transfer fails entirely), validated by control task (selectivity 7.5-18.5pp).\n135\t\n136\t- **Headline result (D032)**: \"Hidden-state probes significantly outperform text probes for safety commitment detection on all 4 models (12/12 Holm-Bonferroni, precision +15-24pp), but this advantage is entirely invisible under biased layer selection metrics. The composite metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically selects shallow layers at 32B scale, masking the HS signal and producing false negatives. This methodological finding, together with model-specific practical trade-offs at 32B (early detection vs broad coverage) and the comprehensive failure of 6 alternative methods, establishes that supervised temporal probing with principled layer selection is the most effective and robust approach for monitoring reasoning-model safety commitment.\"\n137\t\n138\t## Key Evidence Summary\n139\t\n140\t### R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, n=43 test, class_weight=balanced, 60/20/20 split — CANONICAL)\n141\t\n142\tBest layer: 14 (45% depth). Full sweep layers 0-24 confirmed: layer 12 marginally better (+0.4pp bal_acc), no practical difference.\n143\t\n144\t| Method | Coverage | Lead Time | Pre-CP FPR | Safe FPR |\n145\t|--------|----------|-----------|------------|----------|\n146\t| HS Static LR L14 | — | -2.0 | **9.7%** | — |\n147\t| HS Temporal W=5 | — | +0.6 | **10.2%** | — |\n148\t| HS Temporal W=15 | — | **-0.1** | **8.8%** | — |\n149\t| Text Temporal W=15 | — | +10.3* | 20.5% | — |\n150\t| GRU Multi-7L (3-seed) | 72.9+-4.0% | +4.5+-3.0 | 16.5+-4.1% | — |\n151\t\n152\t*Text lead time inflated by high pre-CP FPR (trigger-happy detection)\n153\tNOTE: HS LR lead time collapsed to ~0 with balanced weights — HS detects AT commitment, not before. Only GRU achieves positive lead (+4.5, worst seed +0.9).\n154\t\n155\t### OT-7B (Qwen-7B, STILL-trained, n=47 test, 228 total traces) — D026 CORRECTED to layer 16\n156\t\n157\tBest layer: **16 (57% depth)**. M001 corrected: layer 0 was selected by bal_acc x crossing_rate bug; layer 16 selected by bal_acc.\n158\t\n159\t| Method | Bal Acc | Precision | Coverage | Pre-CP FPR | Lead |\n160\t|--------|---------|-----------|----------|-----------|------|\n161\t| HS Temporal W=3 L16 (best) | **68.8%** | **46.6%** | 53.2% | **10.0%** | — |\n162\t| Text Temporal W=25 (best) | 61.4% | 32.9% | 66.0% | 16.1% | 45.5 |\n163\t| Text Temporal W=3 (matched) | 57.9% | 25.4% | — | 27.1% | — |\n164\t\n165\tBootstrap (layer 16, matched W=3, 1000 resamples):\n166\t   bal_acc +10.7pp, 95% CI [4.9, 16.3], p<0.001 ✅\n167\t   precision +21.9pp, 95% CI [14.0, 29.6], p<0.001 ✅\n168\t   FPR -17.1pp, 95% CI [-22.2, -12.2], p<0.001 ✅ — **3/3 Holm-Bonferroni**\n169\tSelectivity: 18.3pp (real=0.688, random=0.505) — highest across all models\n170\t\n171\t### 32B Cross-Architecture — D029 CORRECTED LAYERS\n172\t\n173\t**QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native, n=19-20, Layer 60 — corrected from L2)**\n174\t- HS vs Text bootstrap (null-centered, 1000 resamples):\n175\t  - Bal Acc: +11-22pp across W=1-15, ALL 15/15 significant (p<0.005)\n176\t  - W=3 (matched): bal_acc +15.4pp (p<0.001 ✅), precision +23.6pp (p<0.001 ✅), FPR -9.8pp (p=0.004 ✅) — **3/3 Holm-Bonferroni**\n177\t- Caveats: HS lead time poor (-1.2 to 1.9 steps vs text 3.7-12.3), position baseline competitive\n178\t- Layer selection: `bal_acc × crossing_rate` selected L2 (3.1%); threshold-dependent metrics consensus selects L60 (94%); pure `bal_acc` selects L30 (47%, bal_acc=0.7513 vs L60=0.7101) (D036)\n179\t\n180\t**R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, n=20, Layer 63 — corrected from L10)**\n181\t- W=1: bal_acc +18.7pp, p<0.001 ✅\n182\t- W≥5: NS (dimensionality: 51200 × W features for n=20)\n183\t- W=1: 3/3 Holm-Bonferroni ✅; W=3: 0/3 Bonferroni (null-centered v2); W≥5: NS\n184\t- Layer selection: `bal_acc × crossing_rate` selected L10 (15.6%); threshold-dependent metrics consensus selects L63 (98%); pure `bal_acc` selects L47 (73%, bal_acc=0.8290 vs L63=0.7585) (D036)\n185\t\n186\t**OLD biased-layer results (RETRACTED as metric artifact):**\n187\t| Model | Biased Layer | Corrected Layer | Biased p-value | Corrected p-value |\n188\t|-------|-------------|----------------|----------------|-------------------|\n189\t| R1-32B | L10 (16%) | L63 (98%) | 0.064-0.13 (NS) | <0.001 (W=1) ✅ |\n190\t| QwQ-32B | L2 (3%) | L60 (94%) | 0.35-0.83 (NS) | <0.005 (all W) ✅ |\n191\t\n192\t### 4-Model Holm-Bonferroni Summary (D032 — COMPLETE: 12/12 ✅)\n193\t\n194\t| Model | Arch | Training | Scale | Layer | W | bal_acc | precision | FPR | Selectivity |\n195\t|-------|------|----------|-------|-------|---|--------|-----------|-----|-------------|\n196\t| R1-8B | Llama | R1-distilled | 8B | L14 (45%) | 15 | +8.2pp ✅ | +22.4pp ✅ | -7.4pp ✅ | 12.5pp |\n197\t| OT-7B | Qwen | STILL | 7B | L16 (57%) | 3 | +10.7pp ✅ | +21.9pp ✅ | -17.1pp ✅ | 18.3pp |\n198\t| QwQ-32B | Qwen | native | 32B | L60 (94%) | 3 | +15.4pp ✅ | +23.6pp ✅ | -9.8pp ✅ | 15.3pp |\n199\t| R1-32B | Qwen | R1-distilled | 32B | L63 (98%) | 1 | +18.7pp ✅ | +15.4pp ✅ | -14.6pp ✅ | 7.5pp |\n200\t\n201\t**12/12 all pass Holm-Bonferroni step-down (α=0.05)**. Previously 5/12 with biased layers.\n202\tPrecision advantage remarkably consistent: +15.4 to +23.6pp across all 4 models.\n203\tCaveats: Different W per model; R1-32B W=1 coverage=25%; QwQ lead time negative.\n204\t\n205\t### Position Mediation Analysis (D038 → D039 → D042 complete)\n206\t\n207\t**Finding**: Position (relative step t/T) is a significant component of probe performance. 3/4 models retain HS advantage after residualization; R1-32B is the exception.\n208\t\n209\t| Model | W | Resid HS BA | Resid Text BA | HS-Text Gap | Gap p | HB |\n210\t|-------|---|------------|--------------|-------------|-------|-----|\n211\t| OT-7B | 3 | 0.609 | 0.540 | **+6.9pp** | <0.001 | ✅ |\n212\t| R1-8B | 15 | 0.669 | 0.605 | **+6.4pp** | <0.001 | ✅ |\n213\t| R1-8B | 3 | 0.571 | 0.534 | **+3.7pp** | <0.001 | ✅ |\n214\t| QwQ-32B | 3 | 0.619 | 0.582 | **+3.7pp** | 0.011 | ✅ |\n215\t| R1-32B | 1 | 0.552 | 0.532 | +1.9pp | 0.914 | ❌ |\n216\t\n217\tPosition-only BA: OT-7B/R1-8B/QwQ ≈ 0.706; R1-32B = 0.686.\n218\t\n219\t**D042 final picture**: HS advantage has two separable components — position-mediated and non-positional. 3/4 models retain significant non-positional HS advantage (HB pass); R1-32B's advantage is almost entirely position-mediated (drop 20.4pp, residualized gap NS). This is consistent with R1-32B's other caveats (W=1 only significant, 25% coverage).\n220\t\n221\t**Key implications for paper**:\n222\t1. Position is a significant component (12-20pp drop) — universal\n223\t2. 3/4 models retain non-positional HS advantage (residualized BA gap +3.7 to +6.9pp, HB pass)\n224\t3. R1-32B is the exception: HS advantage position-mediated, residualized gap NS\n225\t4. Temporal windowing preserves non-positional signal (R1-8B: 24pp drop@W=3 → 16pp@W=15)\n226\t5. Paper framing: \"HS advantage persists after position removal in 3/4 models; R1-32B advantage is position-mediated\"\n227\t\n228\t### 5-Seed Robustness (D041)\n229\t\n230\t| Model | HB Pass Rate | Mean ΔPrec ± std | Mean ΔBA ± std |\n231\t|-------|-------------|------------------|----------------|\n232\t| OT-7B | **5/5** | +20.9 ± 1.3pp | +11.3 ± 1.5pp |\n233\t| R1-32B | **5/5** | +13.7 ± 2.0pp | +11.8 ± 2.4pp |\n234\t| QwQ-32B | **5/5** | +19.9 ± 2.5pp | +11.4 ± 3.2pp |\n235\t| R1-8B | **3/5** | +14.6 ± 4.5pp | +9.4 ± 2.4pp |\n236\t\n237\tR1-8B failures are FPR-specific (seed 123 FPR p=0.262, seed 456 FPR p=0.952). Precision is directionally consistent across all 20 seed-model combinations (always positive). ΔPrec never reverses.\n238\t\n239\t**D041 insight**: Robustness splits by MODEL not SCALE. R1-32B (5/5, ΔPrec std=2.0pp) is more stable than R1-8B (3/5, ΔPrec std=4.5pp). \"7-8B robust / 32B sample-limited\" is factually wrong. R1-32B's absolute precision std (12.8pp) vs paired delta std (2.0pp) — paper must distinguish these.\n240\t\n241\t**Per-model evidence tier (D042)**:\n242\t- **OT-7B**: Cleanest — 5/5 HB, largest residualized gap (+6.9pp), highest selectivity (18.3pp)\n243\t- **QwQ-32B**: Robust — 5/5 HB, residualized gap holds (+3.7pp), but position baseline competitive\n244\t- **R1-8B**: Precision-robust — residualized holds (+3.7-6.4pp), but FPR unstable (3/5 HB)\n245\t- **R1-32B**: Statistically stable (5/5 HB, ΔPrec std=2.0pp) but advantage position-mediated (residualized NS)\n246\t\n247\t**Residualized precision**: Not tested (scope limited to BA per Reviewer recommendation).\n248\t\n249\t### BA-Layer Robustness: Metric Circularity Refuted (D044)\n250\t\n251\tR13 W1 concern: corrected selection uses threshold-dependent metrics (precision, FPR), evaluation also uses precision/FPR — potential circularity. Test: compute HS vs text on BA-selected layers (threshold-free criterion).\n252\t\n253\t| Model | BA-Opt Layer | ΔPrec | ΔBA | ΔFPR | ΔAUROC | All 4 HB? |\n254\t|-------|-------------|-------|-----|------|--------|-----------|\n255\t| R1-8B | L12 | +17.8pp | +7.8pp | -12.9pp | +9.3pp | ✅ |\n256\t| OT-7B | L9 | +25.4pp | +11.7pp | -19.3pp | +14.8pp | ✅ |\n257\t| QwQ-32B | L30 | +18.0pp | +14.8pp | -8.0pp | +17.3pp | ✅ |\n258\t| R1-32B | L47 | +13.7pp | +14.9pp | -23.1pp | +21.1pp | ✅ |\n259\t\n260\t**4/4 models × 4 metrics = 16/16 HB pass.** HS advantage is a genuine modality property, not a layer selection artifact.\n261\t\n262\tKey insight: R1-32B on BA-layer L47 shows ΔPrec +13.7pp and ΔAUROC +21.1pp — stronger than corrected L63 in threshold-free metrics. OT-7B on BA-layer L9 has ΔPrec +25.4pp > corrected L16 (+21.9pp). HS advantage is robust to layer selection criterion.\n263\t\n264\tCaveat: QwQ FPR adj_p=0.041 (marginal, CI close to zero). Other 15/16 tests p≤0.006.\n265\t\n266\t### Post-hoc Power Analysis (D044)\n267\t\n268\t| Model | n (test) | MDE (80% power, α=0.05) |\n269\t|-------|----------|------------------------|\n270\t| OT-7B | 47-69 | 6.7-8.1pp |\n271\t| R1-8B | 43-63 | 7.4-9.0pp |\n272\t| QwQ-32B | 29 | 9.5pp |\n273\t| R1-32B | 20-30 | 8.6-11.2pp |\n274\t\n275\tAll significant effects (15-24pp) are well above MDE → adequately powered. R1-32B residualized gap (+1.9pp) is far below MDE (11.2pp) → NS is power limitation, not evidence of absence.\n276\t\n277\t### Negative Results (expanded with R3 supplementary experiments)\n278\t- **BOCPD/CUSUM (4/4 models)**: R1-8B BOCPD 93% detection but lead +3.5 < threshold +4.9; OT-7B/R1-32B/QwQ all 0% detection across all λ. CUSUM worse than threshold on all models.\n279\t- **Mahalanobis (4/4 models confirmed negative)**: R1-8B (+0.07), OT-7B (+0.031), R1-32B (+0.148), QwQ (+0.204) — all separation ≈ 0. Signal in minor PCA components, not principal directions.\n280\t- **RepE direction probing (4/4 models, corrected layers confirmed)**: Direction-based supervised method (needs CP labels). Results highly layer-dependent and model-specific:\n281\t  - R1-32B L63 (corrected): bal_acc=0.559 (near-random), FPR=49.9% — **direction vector fails at deep layer** where supervised LR excels (0.826). Train-test gap 13.3pp shows poor generalization.\n282\t  - QwQ L60 (corrected): bal_acc=0.756, precision=0.512, FPR=0.309 — **improves** from L2 (0.666), narrows gap with supervised LR W=1 (0.740 bal_acc), but still inferior in precision/FPR.\n283\t  - R1-8B L14: bal_acc=0.749, OT-7B L16: bal_acc=0.642\n284\t  - **Key insight**: Supervised LR is necessary — mean-difference direction cannot decode safety commitment at deep layers for R1-32B (discriminative training finds signal that simple direction averaging misses). Layer selection sensitivity applies to RepE too, but in model-specific ways (R1-32B: shallow better for RepE; QwQ: deep better for both).\n285\t- **MLP nonlinear probe (4/4 models)**: 3/4 models worse than LR. Linear probes sufficient; nonlinearity adds no value.\n286\t- **CCS-adapted (Burns et al. 2023, 4/4 models)**: WEAKLY SUPERVISED (needs CP labels for pair construction, not truly unsupervised). R1-8B bal_acc=0.777 (between supervised LR 0.817 and RepE 0.749). R1-32B FPR=0.410 (near-chance). OT-7B precision=0.319 (near-chance, high lead=20.5 is artifact of high FPR). Uses scheme B layers (biased). Paper must: (a) label as \"weakly supervised\" not \"unsupervised\"; (b) disclose pair construction differs from Burns et al.; (c) note scheme B layer limitation.\n287\t- **Cross-model transfer (exp-003)**: All directions fail entirely (best: 58.3% acc, 40.7% crossing).\n288\t\n289\t## Venue Fit Risks\n290\t\n291\t- **EMNLP LLM Security track fit**: Strong — defense/detection evaluation for reasoning model jailbreak.\n292\t- **Method simplicity risk**: Mitigate: frame as empirical study. Contribution is systematic evaluation + layer selection sensitivity discovery + comprehensive negative results.\n293\t- **Statistical power**: With corrected layers, **12/12** tests pass Holm-Bonferroni across ALL 4 models (up from 5/12 with biased layers).\n294\t- **HS advantage universal**: UPGRADED from \"scale-dependent\" — layer selection is the true moderator, not scale. Cleaner story.\n295\t- **Trace vs step FPR**: Both reported with explanation.\n296\t- **High safe FPR risk**: All methods 39-62% safe FPR. Frame as diagnostic tool limitation.\n297\t- **32B caveats**: HS advantage exists but is more sensitive to window size (R1-32B W=1 only) and affected by dimensionality (51200-d at large W with n=20). Lead times shorter than 7-8B. These are honest limitations, not disqualifiers.\n298\t\n299\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n300\t\n301\t1. \"Method novelty?\" → Empirical study + systematic method comparison + layer selection sensitivity as methodological contribution + comprehensive negative results (6 methods fail).\n302\t2. \"Only one model family?\" → **RESOLVED**: 4 models, 2 architectures (Llama + Qwen), 2 training methods, 2 scales. ALL showing significant HS advantage at corrected layers.\n303\t3. \"GRU unstable?\" → 3-seed reporting with worst-seed bounds. R1-32B GRU at L10 was unstable; needs re-evaluation at L63.\n304\t4. \"Too few traces?\" → Cross-model validation + control task selectivity 7.5-18.5pp.\n305\t5. \"Fine-grained taxonomy?\" → Out of scope for current study.\n306\t6. \"HS doesn't beat text?\" → **QUALIFIED REBUTTAL (D031)**: HS outperforms text at correct layers with STRONG evidence at 7-8B (precision +22pp, Holm-Bonf) and QUALIFIED evidence at 32B (R1-32B: significant W=1 but narrow; QwQ: significant all W but late detection). 32B non-significance at OLD layers was artifact. Layer selection sensitivity analysis shows this is a methodological issue, not absence of signal.\n307\t7. \"Commitment point circular?\" → Cross-model consistency + control task selectivity argues against artifact.\n308\t8. \"Missing related work?\" → Expansion to 30+ citations.\n309\t9. \"No FPR analysis?\" → RESOLVED: both trace-level and step-level FPR reported.\n310\t10. \"High safe FPR?\" → Limitation acknowledged.\n311\t11. \"Control task / position confound?\" → RESOLVED (C2, D011): selectivity 7.5-18.5pp, HS FPR < position-only FPR.\n312\t12. \"Multiple testing correction?\" → **RESOLVED**: 12/12 Holm-Bonferroni step-down (α=0.05) across all 4 models (up from 5/12 with biased layers).\n313\t13. \"Trace vs step FPR?\" → Both reported with explanation.\n314\t14. \"Layer selection is just hyperparameter tuning?\" → No — we show a systematic bias in a commonly used composite metric. The ablation across 5 metrics × 4 models is a reproducible methodological finding, not a one-off parameter choice.\n315\t15. \"Why not just use the best layer for each model?\" → We do. The point is that the METRIC for selecting \"best\" matters. Biased metrics produce systematically wrong selections, masking real effects.\n316\t16. \"32B results are marginal / only W=1 significant for R1-32B?\" → Dimensionality constraint (51200 × W > n=20 at W≥5). QwQ L60 significant at ALL W values. R1-32B L63 significant at W=1. Both confirm HS advantage exists; practical deployment would use W=1-3 for 32B models.\n317\t\n318\t## Title Candidates (D031)\n319\t- \"Layer Selection Matters: How Metric Choice Determines Hidden-State Probe Effectiveness for Safety Monitoring\"\n320\t- \"Temporal Probing for Safety Commitment Detection in Reasoning Models: A Comprehensive Evaluation\"\n321\t- \"What Detects Safety Commitment in Reasoning Models? A Systematic Comparison of Probe-Based Methods\"\n322\t- \"Hidden-State vs Text Monitoring for Reasoning Model Safety: Layer Selection as the Missing Variable\"\n323\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57971aef-fe55-48e4-84c1-753372b1ed10","uuid":"541a8294-5ba3-4aaa-842a-08b1445e5906","timestamp":"2026-05-21T21:57:38.118Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T212242/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — contrastive_trace_safety\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (LLM Security Track)\n- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026\n\n## Core Claims (D031: layer selection as primary contribution, 4 models)\n\n**Contribution structure (D031)**:\n- **Primary (methodological)**: Layer selection metric sensitivity — biased metrics reverse conclusions\n- **Secondary (empirical)**: HS advantage at correct layers, qualified by scale; temporal improvement universal\n- **Tertiary (negative results)**: 6 alternative methods fail, establishing what doesn't work\n\n1. **Temporal context universally improves probe accuracy across both modalities** (D014 + D023 nuanced) — Temporal context is the most robust improvement across all 4 models and both modalities. Text probes benefit consistently from larger windows across all models. HS probes benefit in 3/4 models; OT-7B HS peaks at W=3 and declines at larger W (confirmed on both layer 0 and layer 16).\n\n   **HS temporal gains** (W=1→best, 3 original models): accuracy +3pp (77.8%→81.0%), precision +13pp (69.3%→82.2%), FPR halved (9.65%→4.83%).\n   **OT-7B HS temporal** (layer 16): bal_acc peaks at W=3 (68.8%), declines to 65.9% at W=25 (-2.9pp). BUT precision and FPR continue improving (prec 42.8%→49.4%, FPR 12.0%→7.5%). Temporal saturation is bal_acc-specific; larger W trades bal_acc for better precision/FPR. Confirmed on both layer 0 and layer 16 — genuine OT-7B characteristic.\n   **Text temporal gains** (all 4 models, W=1→best): universally +3-12pp bal_acc, +8-23pp precision, FPR reduced.\n\n   **IMPORTANT: coverage direction depends on detection criterion K.** K=1 (single crossing): coverage DECREASES with W. K=5 (consecutive crossings): coverage may INCREASE with W. Claim 1 is about accuracy/precision improvement, NOT coverage.\n\n   The core insight: \"Temporal context universally improves text probes across all 4 models. For HS probes, 3/4 models benefit from larger temporal windows, while OT-7B shows early saturation (W=3 peak).\"\n\n2. **HS probes outperform text probes at correctly selected layers, with strong evidence at 7-8B and model-specific patterns at 32B** (D031: qualified from D030 \"universal\") —\n\n   **KEY FINDING (D029)**: The apparent \"scale-dependent\" HS advantage (significant at 7-8B, absent at 32B) was an artifact of the layer selection metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate`, which systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models. At correctly selected deep layers, HS outperforms text on all 4 models, but the NATURE of the advantage differs by scale.\n\n   **Layer selection metric bias (D028, refined D036)**: `bal_acc × crossing_rate` selects shallow layers because shallow layers have higher crossing rates (more trigger-happy probes). 3/4 non-biased threshold-dependent metrics (precision, pre_cp_fpr, composite) converge on deep layers; pure bal_acc (threshold-free) selects middle layers (L30/L47). Deployment-relevant metric consensus selects L60/L63 for 32B models.\n\n   **7-8B models: HS significantly outperforms text (2/2 models) — UNCHANGED**\n\n   **R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, n=43):**\n   - Precision: 69.7% vs 47.2% (+22.4pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n   - Accuracy: 81.3% vs 72.9% (+8.2pp, p=0.005, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n   - Pre-CP FPR: 14.8% vs 22.2% (-7.4pp, p=0.044, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅** — rank 12/12, threshold=0.0500)\n\n   **OT-7B (Qwen-7B, STILL-trained, n=47, layer 16, matched W=3) — D026 M001 corrected:**\n   - Bal Acc: 68.8% vs 58.1% (+10.7pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n   - Precision: 46.6% vs 24.7% (+21.9pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n   - Pre-CP FPR: 10.0% vs 27.1% (-17.1pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n   - **3/3 metrics significant — OT-7B cleanest single-model evidence**\n\n   **32B models: HS outperforms text at CORRECTED LAYERS but with model-specific caveats (D029+D031)**\n\n   **QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native, n=19-20, Layer 60 corrected from L2):**\n   - Bal Acc: +11-22pp across all 15 window sizes, all p<0.005 (bootstrap, null-centered)\n   - 15/15 W values show significant HS advantage\n   - **CRITICAL CAVEAT (D031)**: HS lead time NEGATIVE (-1.2 to 1.9 steps vs text 3.7-12.3). HS is \"more accurate but detects later.\" Position baseline bal_acc=0.746 > HS=0.740. In safety deployment, detecting 8-12 steps earlier may outweigh higher accuracy.\n   - W=3: bal_acc +15.4pp, precision +23.6pp, FPR -9.8pp — **3/3 Holm-Bonferroni ✅**\n\n   **R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, n=20, Layer 63 corrected from L10):**\n   - W=1: bal_acc +18.7pp (0.826 vs 0.639), p=0.000; precision +15.4pp (0.603 vs 0.448), p=0.000; FPR -14.6pp (0.235 vs 0.380), p=0.007 — **3/3 Bonferroni ✅**\n   - W=3: bal_acc +11.2pp p=0.020, precision p=0.032, FPR p=0.077 — **0/3 Bonferroni** (v2 null-centered)\n   - W=5-15: all NS\n   - **CRITICAL CAVEAT (D031)**: Only W=1 passes Bonferroni, coverage=25% (5/20 traces). Evidence statistically narrow.\n   - **POSITIVE**: Lead time +11.7-13.3 steps (excellent early warning). HS >> Position (+14.2pp).\n   - **NOTE**: L10 vs L63 at W=10: L10=0.795 vs L63=0.754 — L10 slightly better. \"Scheme B选错层\" narrative weaker for R1-32B.\n   - ✅ P-VALUE UNIFIED: null-centered two-sided, consistent with QwQ.\n\n   **32B cross-model divergence (D031 key finding for Discussion):**\n   | Dimension | R1-32B L63 | QwQ L60 |\n   |-----------|-----------|---------|\n   | Bonferroni | W=1 only (3/3) | All 15 W (3/3) |\n   | Lead time | +11.7 to +13.3 (excellent) | -1.2 to +1.9 (poor) |\n   | HS vs Position | HS >> Pos (+14.2pp) | HS < Pos (-0.6pp) |\n   | Coverage (W=1) | 25% | 74% |\n   Paper MUST discuss this divergence per-model, not unify into single narrative.\n\n   **OLD 32B results at BIASED layers (L2/L10) — RETRACTED as metric artifact:**\n   - R1-32B L10: precision +9.4pp (p=0.064), accuracy +6.2pp (p=0.13)\n   - QwQ-32B L2: precision +5.8pp (p=0.35), accuracy +0.9pp (p=0.83)\n   - These non-significant results were caused by the biased `bal_acc × crossing_rate` metric selecting layers where HS signal is weak.\n\n   **Holm-Bonferroni COMPLETE (D032): 12/12 ALL PASS**\n   Previously 5/12 (biased layers). With corrected layers: **12/12** step-down pass (α=0.05).\n\n   | Rank | Model | Metric | p-value | Threshold | Pass |\n   |------|-------|--------|---------|-----------|------|\n   | 1 | R1-8B | precision | <0.001 | 0.0042 | ✅ |\n   | 2 | OT-7B | bal_acc | <0.001 | 0.0045 | ✅ |\n   | 3 | OT-7B | precision | <0.001 | 0.0050 | ✅ |\n   | 4 | OT-7B | FPR | <0.001 | 0.0056 | ✅ |\n   | 5 | QwQ | bal_acc | <0.001 | 0.0063 | ✅ |\n   | 6 | QwQ | precision | <0.001 | 0.0071 | ✅ |\n   | 7 | R1-32B | bal_acc | <0.001 | 0.0083 | ✅ |\n   | 8 | R1-32B | precision | <0.001 | 0.0100 | ✅ |\n   | 9 | QwQ | FPR | 0.004 | 0.0125 | ✅ |\n   | 10 | R1-8B | bal_acc | 0.005 | 0.0167 | ✅ |\n   | 11 | R1-32B | FPR | 0.007 | 0.0250 | ✅ |\n   | 12 | R1-8B | FPR | 0.044 | 0.0500 | ✅ |\n\n   **Caveats**: Different W per model (R1-8B W=15, OT-7B/QwQ W=3, R1-32B W=1). Tests measure \"HS > text at each model's best setting,\" not \"at uniform W.\" R1-32B W=1 has 25% coverage. Paper must state these conditions.\n\n   **FPR dual reporting**: Step-level (r1-8b: HS 8.8%, text 20.5%) vs trace-level (HS 14.8%, text 22.2%). Both reported.\n\n   **FPR reversal RESOLVED (D026)**: OT-7B HS FPR@L16 (10.0%) < Text FPR at ANY window (16.1%). HS dominates on FPR at 7-8B.\n\n   **Effect size consistency**: Precision advantage across all 4 models at corrected layers: R1-8B +22.4pp, OT-7B +21.2pp, QwQ +23.6pp, R1-32B +15.4pp. Remarkably consistent +15-24pp range.\n\n   Paper recommended claim (D032): \"At correctly selected layers, HS probes significantly outperform text probes on all 4 models (12/12 Holm-Bonferroni, precision +15-24pp). The layer selection metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically biases toward shallow layers, entirely masking this advantage at 32B scale. Practical implications differ by model: 7-8B models show strong HS advantage with comparable timing; R1-32B shows excellent lead time (+13 steps) but narrow coverage at the significant window; QwQ shows robust statistical significance but HS detects later than text.\"\n\n3. **HS and text monitoring exhibit different detection timing profiles** (narrowed from old Claim 3, now 4/4 models with GRU) — On r1-8b: HS temporal LR detects AT the commitment point (lead ~0) while text detects ~10 steps earlier. GRU achieves modest positive lead (+4.5+-3.0, worst seed +0.9). BOCPD/CUSUM fail to improve over simple thresholds. The oracle's +31-step advantage shows hidden states encode current safety state but poorly predict future trajectory. On 32B models: timing profiles vary (QwQ GRU lead +15.9, R1-32B +8.8) but FPR differences are non-significant. OT-7B GRU (layer 16, multi-layer L13-19, 3 seeds): bal_acc 74.6+-1.3%, coverage 64.5+-7.0%, lead +33.6+-4.8, FPR 27.4+-6.1%. GRU trades higher FPR for longer lead (consistent with R1-8B pattern). NOTE: OT-7B absolute lead times are inflated by long traces (~80 sentences vs ~30). Paper should normalize or explicitly caveat trace length differences when comparing across models.\n\n   NOTE: 32B timing profiles should be RE-EVALUATED at corrected layers (L60/L63) — current GRU results used old biased layers.\n\n## Methodological Contribution: Layer Selection Sensitivity (D028, elevated from RETRACTED Claim 4)\n\nThe layer selection metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically biases toward shallow layers because shallow layers have higher crossing rates (more frequently crossing the detection threshold). This bias is especially problematic for 32B models, where it selects layers at 3-16% depth instead of deep layers selected by threshold-dependent metrics.\n\n**Ablation evidence (4 models × 5 metrics) — UPDATED D036**:\n- `bal_acc × crossing_rate`: selects L2 (QwQ), L10 (R1-32B) — shallow, biased\n- Pure `bal_acc`: selects **middle layers** for 32B — L30 (QwQ, 47%), L47 (R1-32B, 73%); deep for 7-8B (agrees with other metrics). Threshold-free metric, behaves like AUROC.\n- `precision`: selects deep layers for all models (L60/L63 for 32B)\n- `pre_cp_fpr`: selects deep layers for all models\n- `composite` (additive): selects deep layers for all models\n- **Pattern**: Threshold-FREE metrics (bal_acc, AUROC) → middle layers; threshold-DEPENDENT metrics (precision, FPR, composite) → deep layers. Crossing_rate-contaminated metrics → shallow layers.\n\n**LOO-CV stability (D036)**: Under LOO-CV, the dominant layer is selected in 65-68% of folds regardless of metric. QwQ L30 selected 13/19 folds (bal_acc), R1-32B L47 selected 13/20 folds (bal_acc). Selection is robust to val set composition.\n\n**Corrected layer definition (D036)**: \"Corrected layers\" = consensus of threshold-dependent metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) — 3 of 4 non-biased metrics, not all 4. Pure bal_acc selects middle layers (L30/L47) with higher step-level accuracy (0.75/0.83 vs 0.71/0.76 at deep layers), but deployment-relevant metrics (precision, FPR) favor deep layers where all 4 models achieve HB-significant HS advantage.\n\n**Impact**: Incorrect layer selection entirely masked the HS advantage at 32B scale, leading to the false conclusion that HS advantage is scale-dependent. Even among unbiased metrics, metric choice affects optimal layer depth — threshold-free vs threshold-dependent is a second dimension of researcher degrees of freedom beyond the crossing_rate bias.\n\n**Anti-circular-reasoning defense (D031, refined D036)**: The claim that deep layers are \"correct\" for deployment rests on: (1) `crossing_rate` has a MECHANISTIC bias — shallow layers have compressed representations (1.4-2.7× smaller cosine distances) generating noise-driven crossings; (2) threshold-dependent metrics (precision, FPR) directly measure deployment-relevant properties; (3) at deep layers, all 4 models show significant HS advantage (HB corrected); (4) LOO-CV confirms selection stability. The paper presents layer selection as a \"researcher degree of freedom\" with systematic audit via sensitivity analysis.\n\n**Paper treatment**: Dedicated subsection in Method + Table showing metric × model → selected layer + downstream HS-text comparison. Frame as sensitivity analysis, not \"correct vs incorrect.\"\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: Reasoning models exhibit \"self-jailbreak\" — safety reasoning and harmful execution interleave throughout extended CoT, culminating in a \"commitment point\" where safety reasoning permanently ceases. Existing hidden-state detectors (TrajGuard, SafeSwitch, SIREN) target adversarial INPUT attacks on instruction-tuned models; they have never been evaluated on reasoning-model self-jailbreak where the threat is the model's OWN reasoning process.\n\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Hidden-state detectors achieve 95% defense on standard LLMs (TrajGuard) but operate at token-level. Reasoning-model safety analysis (Self-Jailbreaking, HarmThoughts) characterizes the phenomenon without providing deployable detectors. Text-level CoT monitors (SafeRemind, SafeThink) are vulnerable to pathological CoT. No prior work systematically compares detection modalities for reasoning-model self-jailbreak.\n\n- **Our framing**: We provide the first systematic evaluation of detection methods for reasoning-model safety commitment across **4 models, 2 architectures (Llama + Qwen), 2 training methods (R1-distilled + STILL), and 2 scales (7-8B + 32B)**, comparing hidden-state probes, text-based probes, sequence models (GRU), change-point detection (BOCPD/CUSUM), unsupervised methods (Mahalanobis, RepE direction, CCS), and nonlinear probes (MLP). We discover that: (a) temporal context is the most robust improvement, benefiting both modalities universally; (b) HS probes universally outperform text at correctly selected layers, with a critical sensitivity to layer selection methodology; (c) existing methods designed for other settings (TrajGuard, BOCPD, CCS, RepE) fail on reasoning model commitment; (d) linear probes are sufficient (MLP adds no value); (e) safety representations are model-specific (cross-transfer fails entirely), validated by control task (selectivity 7.5-18.5pp).\n\n- **Headline result (D032)**: \"Hidden-state probes significantly outperform text probes for safety commitment detection on all 4 models (12/12 Holm-Bonferroni, precision +15-24pp), but this advantage is entirely invisible under biased layer selection metrics. The composite metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically selects shallow layers at 32B scale, masking the HS signal and producing false negatives. This methodological finding, together with model-specific practical trade-offs at 32B (early detection vs broad coverage) and the comprehensive failure of 6 alternative methods, establishes that supervised temporal probing with principled layer selection is the most effective and robust approach for monitoring reasoning-model safety commitment.\"\n\n## Key Evidence Summary\n\n### R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, n=43 test, class_weight=balanced, 60/20/20 split — CANONICAL)\n\nBest layer: 14 (45% depth). Full sweep layers 0-24 confirmed: layer 12 marginally better (+0.4pp bal_acc), no practical difference.\n\n| Method | Coverage | Lead Time | Pre-CP FPR | Safe FPR |\n|--------|----------|-----------|------------|----------|\n| HS Static LR L14 | — | -2.0 | **9.7%** | — |\n| HS Temporal W=5 | — | +0.6 | **10.2%** | — |\n| HS Temporal W=15 | — | **-0.1** | **8.8%** | — |\n| Text Temporal W=15 | — | +10.3* | 20.5% | — |\n| GRU Multi-7L (3-seed) | 72.9+-4.0% | +4.5+-3.0 | 16.5+-4.1% | — |\n\n*Text lead time inflated by high pre-CP FPR (trigger-happy detection)\nNOTE: HS LR lead time collapsed to ~0 with balanced weights — HS detects AT commitment, not before. Only GRU achieves positive lead (+4.5, worst seed +0.9).\n\n### OT-7B (Qwen-7B, STILL-trained, n=47 test, 228 total traces) — D026 CORRECTED to layer 16\n\nBest layer: **16 (57% depth)**. M001 corrected: layer 0 was selected by bal_acc x crossing_rate bug; layer 16 selected by bal_acc.\n\n| Method | Bal Acc | Precision | Coverage | Pre-CP FPR | Lead |\n|--------|---------|-----------|----------|-----------|------|\n| HS Temporal W=3 L16 (best) | **68.8%** | **46.6%** | 53.2% | **10.0%** | — |\n| Text Temporal W=25 (best) | 61.4% | 32.9% | 66.0% | 16.1% | 45.5 |\n| Text Temporal W=3 (matched) | 57.9% | 25.4% | — | 27.1% | — |\n\nBootstrap (layer 16, matched W=3, 1000 resamples):\n   bal_acc +10.7pp, 95% CI [4.9, 16.3], p<0.001 ✅\n   precision +21.9pp, 95% CI [14.0, 29.6], p<0.001 ✅\n   FPR -17.1pp, 95% CI [-22.2, -12.2], p<0.001 ✅ — **3/3 Holm-Bonferroni**\nSelectivity: 18.3pp (real=0.688, random=0.505) — highest across all models\n\n### 32B Cross-Architecture — D029 CORRECTED LAYERS\n\n**QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native, n=19-20, Layer 60 — corrected from L2)**\n- HS vs Text bootstrap (null-centered, 1000 resamples):\n  - Bal Acc: +11-22pp across W=1-15, ALL 15/15 significant (p<0.005)\n  - W=3 (matched): bal_acc +15.4pp (p<0.001 ✅), precision +23.6pp (p<0.001 ✅), FPR -9.8pp (p=0.004 ✅) — **3/3 Holm-Bonferroni**\n- Caveats: HS lead time poor (-1.2 to 1.9 steps vs text 3.7-12.3), position baseline competitive\n- Layer selection: `bal_acc × crossing_rate` selected L2 (3.1%); threshold-dependent metrics consensus selects L60 (94%); pure `bal_acc` selects L30 (47%, bal_acc=0.7513 vs L60=0.7101) (D036)\n\n**R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, n=20, Layer 63 — corrected from L10)**\n- W=1: bal_acc +18.7pp, p<0.001 ✅\n- W≥5: NS (dimensionality: 51200 × W features for n=20)\n- W=1: 3/3 Holm-Bonferroni ✅; W=3: 0/3 Bonferroni (null-centered v2); W≥5: NS\n- Layer selection: `bal_acc × crossing_rate` selected L10 (15.6%); threshold-dependent metrics consensus selects L63 (98%); pure `bal_acc` selects L47 (73%, bal_acc=0.8290 vs L63=0.7585) (D036)\n\n**OLD biased-layer results (RETRACTED as metric artifact):**\n| Model | Biased Layer | Corrected Layer | Biased p-value | Corrected p-value |\n|-------|-------------|----------------|----------------|-------------------|\n| R1-32B | L10 (16%) | L63 (98%) | 0.064-0.13 (NS) | <0.001 (W=1) ✅ |\n| QwQ-32B | L2 (3%) | L60 (94%) | 0.35-0.83 (NS) | <0.005 (all W) ✅ |\n\n### 4-Model Holm-Bonferroni Summary (D032 — COMPLETE: 12/12 ✅)\n\n| Model | Arch | Training | Scale | Layer | W | bal_acc | precision | FPR | Selectivity |\n|-------|------|----------|-------|-------|---|--------|-----------|-----|-------------|\n| R1-8B | Llama | R1-distilled | 8B | L14 (45%) | 15 | +8.2pp ✅ | +22.4pp ✅ | -7.4pp ✅ | 12.5pp |\n| OT-7B | Qwen | STILL | 7B | L16 (57%) | 3 | +10.7pp ✅ | +21.9pp ✅ | -17.1pp ✅ | 18.3pp |\n| QwQ-32B | Qwen | native | 32B | L60 (94%) | 3 | +15.4pp ✅ | +23.6pp ✅ | -9.8pp ✅ | 15.3pp |\n| R1-32B | Qwen | R1-distilled | 32B | L63 (98%) | 1 | +18.7pp ✅ | +15.4pp ✅ | -14.6pp ✅ | 7.5pp |\n\n**12/12 all pass Holm-Bonferroni step-down (α=0.05)**. Previously 5/12 with biased layers.\nPrecision advantage remarkably consistent: +15.4 to +23.6pp across all 4 models.\nCaveats: Different W per model; R1-32B W=1 coverage=25%; QwQ lead time negative.\n\n### Position Mediation Analysis (D038 → D039 → D042 complete)\n\n**Finding**: Position (relative step t/T) is a significant component of probe performance. 3/4 models retain HS advantage after residualization; R1-32B is the exception.\n\n| Model | W | Resid HS BA | Resid Text BA | HS-Text Gap | Gap p | HB |\n|-------|---|------------|--------------|-------------|-------|-----|\n| OT-7B | 3 | 0.609 | 0.540 | **+6.9pp** | <0.001 | ✅ |\n| R1-8B | 15 | 0.669 | 0.605 | **+6.4pp** | <0.001 | ✅ |\n| R1-8B | 3 | 0.571 | 0.534 | **+3.7pp** | <0.001 | ✅ |\n| QwQ-32B | 3 | 0.619 | 0.582 | **+3.7pp** | 0.011 | ✅ |\n| R1-32B | 1 | 0.552 | 0.532 | +1.9pp | 0.914 | ❌ |\n\nPosition-only BA: OT-7B/R1-8B/QwQ ≈ 0.706; R1-32B = 0.686.\n\n**D042 final picture**: HS advantage has two separable components — position-mediated and non-positional. 3/4 models retain significant non-positional HS advantage (HB pass); R1-32B's advantage is almost entirely position-mediated (drop 20.4pp, residualized gap NS). This is consistent with R1-32B's other caveats (W=1 only significant, 25% coverage).\n\n**Key implications for paper**:\n1. Position is a significant component (12-20pp drop) — universal\n2. 3/4 models retain non-positional HS advantage (residualized BA gap +3.7 to +6.9pp, HB pass)\n3. R1-32B is the exception: HS advantage position-mediated, residualized gap NS\n4. Temporal windowing preserves non-positional signal (R1-8B: 24pp drop@W=3 → 16pp@W=15)\n5. Paper framing: \"HS advantage persists after position removal in 3/4 models; R1-32B advantage is position-mediated\"\n\n### 5-Seed Robustness (D041)\n\n| Model | HB Pass Rate | Mean ΔPrec ± std | Mean ΔBA ± std |\n|-------|-------------|------------------|----------------|\n| OT-7B | **5/5** | +20.9 ± 1.3pp | +11.3 ± 1.5pp |\n| R1-32B | **5/5** | +13.7 ± 2.0pp | +11.8 ± 2.4pp |\n| QwQ-32B | **5/5** | +19.9 ± 2.5pp | +11.4 ± 3.2pp |\n| R1-8B | **3/5** | +14.6 ± 4.5pp | +9.4 ± 2.4pp |\n\nR1-8B failures are FPR-specific (seed 123 FPR p=0.262, seed 456 FPR p=0.952). Precision is directionally consistent across all 20 seed-model combinations (always positive). ΔPrec never reverses.\n\n**D041 insight**: Robustness splits by MODEL not SCALE. R1-32B (5/5, ΔPrec std=2.0pp) is more stable than R1-8B (3/5, ΔPrec std=4.5pp). \"7-8B robust / 32B sample-limited\" is factually wrong. R1-32B's absolute precision std (12.8pp) vs paired delta std (2.0pp) — paper must distinguish these.\n\n**Per-model evidence tier (D042)**:\n- **OT-7B**: Cleanest — 5/5 HB, largest residualized gap (+6.9pp), highest selectivity (18.3pp)\n- **QwQ-32B**: Robust — 5/5 HB, residualized gap holds (+3.7pp), but position baseline competitive\n- **R1-8B**: Precision-robust — residualized holds (+3.7-6.4pp), but FPR unstable (3/5 HB)\n- **R1-32B**: Statistically stable (5/5 HB, ΔPrec std=2.0pp) but advantage position-mediated (residualized NS)\n\n**Residualized precision**: Not tested (scope limited to BA per Reviewer recommendation).\n\n### BA-Layer Robustness: Metric Circularity Refuted (D044)\n\nR13 W1 concern: corrected selection uses threshold-dependent metrics (precision, FPR), evaluation also uses precision/FPR — potential circularity. Test: compute HS vs text on BA-selected layers (threshold-free criterion).\n\n| Model | BA-Opt Layer | ΔPrec | ΔBA | ΔFPR | ΔAUROC | All 4 HB? |\n|-------|-------------|-------|-----|------|--------|-----------|\n| R1-8B | L12 | +17.8pp | +7.8pp | -12.9pp | +9.3pp | ✅ |\n| OT-7B | L9 | +25.4pp | +11.7pp | -19.3pp | +14.8pp | ✅ |\n| QwQ-32B | L30 | +18.0pp | +14.8pp | -8.0pp | +17.3pp | ✅ |\n| R1-32B | L47 | +13.7pp | +14.9pp | -23.1pp | +21.1pp | ✅ |\n\n**4/4 models × 4 metrics = 16/16 HB pass.** HS advantage is a genuine modality property, not a layer selection artifact.\n\nKey insight: R1-32B on BA-layer L47 shows ΔPrec +13.7pp and ΔAUROC +21.1pp — stronger than corrected L63 in threshold-free metrics. OT-7B on BA-layer L9 has ΔPrec +25.4pp > corrected L16 (+21.9pp). HS advantage is robust to layer selection criterion.\n\nCaveat: QwQ FPR adj_p=0.041 (marginal, CI close to zero). Other 15/16 tests p≤0.006.\n\n### Post-hoc Power Analysis (D044)\n\n| Model | n (test) | MDE (80% power, α=0.05) |\n|-------|----------|------------------------|\n| OT-7B | 47-69 | 6.7-8.1pp |\n| R1-8B | 43-63 | 7.4-9.0pp |\n| QwQ-32B | 29 | 9.5pp |\n| R1-32B | 20-30 | 8.6-11.2pp |\n\nAll significant effects (15-24pp) are well above MDE → adequately powered. R1-32B residualized gap (+1.9pp) is far below MDE (11.2pp) → NS is power limitation, not evidence of absence.\n\n### Negative Results (expanded with R3 supplementary experiments)\n- **BOCPD/CUSUM (4/4 models)**: R1-8B BOCPD 93% detection but lead +3.5 < threshold +4.9; OT-7B/R1-32B/QwQ all 0% detection across all λ. CUSUM worse than threshold on all models.\n- **Mahalanobis (4/4 models confirmed negative)**: R1-8B (+0.07), OT-7B (+0.031), R1-32B (+0.148), QwQ (+0.204) — all separation ≈ 0. Signal in minor PCA components, not principal directions.\n- **RepE direction probing (4/4 models, corrected layers confirmed)**: Direction-based supervised method (needs CP labels). Results highly layer-dependent and model-specific:\n  - R1-32B L63 (corrected): bal_acc=0.559 (near-random), FPR=49.9% — **direction vector fails at deep layer** where supervised LR excels (0.826). Train-test gap 13.3pp shows poor generalization.\n  - QwQ L60 (corrected): bal_acc=0.756, precision=0.512, FPR=0.309 — **improves** from L2 (0.666), narrows gap with supervised LR W=1 (0.740 bal_acc), but still inferior in precision/FPR.\n  - R1-8B L14: bal_acc=0.749, OT-7B L16: bal_acc=0.642\n  - **Key insight**: Supervised LR is necessary — mean-difference direction cannot decode safety commitment at deep layers for R1-32B (discriminative training finds signal that simple direction averaging misses). Layer selection sensitivity applies to RepE too, but in model-specific ways (R1-32B: shallow better for RepE; QwQ: deep better for both).\n- **MLP nonlinear probe (4/4 models)**: 3/4 models worse than LR. Linear probes sufficient; nonlinearity adds no value.\n- **CCS-adapted (Burns et al. 2023, 4/4 models)**: WEAKLY SUPERVISED (needs CP labels for pair construction, not truly unsupervised). R1-8B bal_acc=0.777 (between supervised LR 0.817 and RepE 0.749). R1-32B FPR=0.410 (near-chance). OT-7B precision=0.319 (near-chance, high lead=20.5 is artifact of high FPR). Uses scheme B layers (biased). Paper must: (a) label as \"weakly supervised\" not \"unsupervised\"; (b) disclose pair construction differs from Burns et al.; (c) note scheme B layer limitation.\n- **Cross-model transfer (exp-003)**: All directions fail entirely (best: 58.3% acc, 40.7% crossing).\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n- **EMNLP LLM Security track fit**: Strong — defense/detection evaluation for reasoning model jailbreak.\n- **Method simplicity risk**: Mitigate: frame as empirical study. Contribution is systematic evaluation + layer selection sensitivity discovery + comprehensive negative results.\n- **Statistical power**: With corrected layers, **12/12** tests pass Holm-Bonferroni across ALL 4 models (up from 5/12 with biased layers).\n- **HS advantage universal**: UPGRADED from \"scale-dependent\" — layer selection is the true moderator, not scale. Cleaner story.\n- **Trace vs step FPR**: Both reported with explanation.\n- **High safe FPR risk**: All methods 39-62% safe FPR. Frame as diagnostic tool limitation.\n- **32B caveats**: HS advantage exists but is more sensitive to window size (R1-32B W=1 only) and affected by dimensionality (51200-d at large W with n=20). Lead times shorter than 7-8B. These are honest limitations, not disqualifiers.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n1. \"Method novelty?\" → Empirical study + systematic method comparison + layer selection sensitivity as methodological contribution + comprehensive negative results (6 methods fail).\n2. \"Only one model family?\" → **RESOLVED**: 4 models, 2 architectures (Llama + Qwen), 2 training methods, 2 scales. ALL showing significant HS advantage at corrected layers.\n3. \"GRU unstable?\" → 3-seed reporting with worst-seed bounds. R1-32B GRU at L10 was unstable; needs re-evaluation at L63.\n4. \"Too few traces?\" → Cross-model validation + control task selectivity 7.5-18.5pp.\n5. \"Fine-grained taxonomy?\" → Out of scope for current study.\n6. \"HS doesn't beat text?\" → **QUALIFIED REBUTTAL (D031)**: HS outperforms text at correct layers with STRONG evidence at 7-8B (precision +22pp, Holm-Bonf) and QUALIFIED evidence at 32B (R1-32B: significant W=1 but narrow; QwQ: significant all W but late detection). 32B non-significance at OLD layers was artifact. Layer selection sensitivity analysis shows this is a methodological issue, not absence of signal.\n7. \"Commitment point circular?\" → Cross-model consistency + control task selectivity argues against artifact.\n8. \"Missing related work?\" → Expansion to 30+ citations.\n9. \"No FPR analysis?\" → RESOLVED: both trace-level and step-level FPR reported.\n10. \"High safe FPR?\" → Limitation acknowledged.\n11. \"Control task / position confound?\" → RESOLVED (C2, D011): selectivity 7.5-18.5pp, HS FPR < position-only FPR.\n12. \"Multiple testing correction?\" → **RESOLVED**: 12/12 Holm-Bonferroni step-down (α=0.05) across all 4 models (up from 5/12 with biased layers).\n13. \"Trace vs step FPR?\" → Both reported with explanation.\n14. \"Layer selection is just hyperparameter tuning?\" → No — we show a systematic bias in a commonly used composite metric. The ablation across 5 metrics × 4 models is a reproducible methodological finding, not a one-off parameter choice.\n15. \"Why not just use the best layer for each model?\" → We do. The point is that the METRIC for selecting \"best\" matters. Biased metrics produce systematically wrong selections, masking real effects.\n16. \"32B results are marginal / only W=1 significant for R1-32B?\" → Dimensionality constraint (51200 × W > n=20 at W≥5). QwQ L60 significant at ALL W values. R1-32B L63 significant at W=1. Both confirm HS advantage exists; practical deployment would use W=1-3 for 32B models.\n\n## Title Candidates (D031)\n- \"Layer Selection Matters: How Metric Choice Determines Hidden-State Probe Effectiveness for Safety Monitoring\"\n- \"Temporal Probing for Safety Commitment Detection in Reasoning Models: A Comprehensive Evaluation\"\n- \"What Detects Safety Commitment in Reasoning Models? A Systematic Comparison of Probe-Based Methods\"\n- \"Hidden-State vs Text Monitoring for Reasoning Model Safety: Layer Selection as the Missing Variable\"\n","numLines":323,"startLine":1,"totalLines":323}}}
